WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:07.886 --> 00:13.116 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Powell's
Press Conference September 26,

00:13.116 --> 00:15.976 align:middle
2018 CHAIRMAN POWELL:
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:15.976 --> 00:16.646 align:middle
Thanks for being here.

00:16.716 --> 00:19.596 align:middle
I am going to start with an
overview of how my colleagues

00:19.596 --> 00:23.086 align:middle
and I see the economy, what we
decided today, and then a bit

00:23.086 --> 00:25.186 align:middle
about our expectations
for the months ahead.

00:25.736 --> 00:28.296 align:middle
Our economy is strong.

00:29.286 --> 00:31.196 align:middle
Growth is running
at a healthy clip.

00:31.976 --> 00:33.536 align:middle
Unemployment is low, the number

00:33.536 --> 00:37.346 align:middle
of people working is rising
steadily, and wages are up.

00:38.246 --> 00:39.626 align:middle
Inflation is low and stable.

00:40.716 --> 00:42.516 align:middle
All of these are
very good signs.

00:43.316 --> 00:45.286 align:middle
Of course, that's not to say
that everything is perfect.

00:45.896 --> 00:48.536 align:middle
The benefits of this strong
economy have not reached

00:48.596 --> 00:49.426 align:middle
all Americans.

00:50.376 --> 00:53.176 align:middle
Many of our country's economic
challenges are beyond the scope

00:53.176 --> 00:55.886 align:middle
of the Fed, but my colleagues
and I are doing all we can

00:55.886 --> 00:58.846 align:middle
to keep the economy strong,
healthy, and moving forward.

00:59.866 --> 01:02.076 align:middle
That is the best way we
can promote an environment

01:02.076 --> 01:04.796 align:middle
in which every American has
the opportunity to succeed.

01:05.676 --> 01:09.566 align:middle
Each time my colleagues and I
meet, we face the same question:

01:09.886 --> 01:13.826 align:middle
How can we set policy to
best support job growth

01:13.826 --> 01:15.356 align:middle
and low, steady inflation?

01:16.156 --> 01:18.806 align:middle
For many years, this
question called

01:18.806 --> 01:21.016 align:middle
for very low interest
rates to help an economy

01:21.016 --> 01:23.636 align:middle
that had been damaged by
the deep financial crisis

01:23.636 --> 01:25.126 align:middle
that gripped the
world 10 years ago.

01:26.316 --> 01:28.236 align:middle
As the economy has
steadily gained strength,

01:28.396 --> 01:31.216 align:middle
the Fed has been gradually
returning interest rates closer

01:31.496 --> 01:33.476 align:middle
to levels that are normal
in a healthy economy.

01:34.656 --> 01:36.476 align:middle
We took another step
on that path today

01:36.526 --> 01:39.416 align:middle
with a quarter-point increase
in short-term interest rates.

01:40.656 --> 01:43.346 align:middle
These rates remain low, and
my colleagues and I believe

01:43.386 --> 01:45.756 align:middle
that this gradual return
to normal is helping

01:45.756 --> 01:47.886 align:middle
to sustain this strong economy

01:47.936 --> 01:50.626 align:middle
for the longer-run
benefit of all Americans.

01:51.416 --> 01:55.816 align:middle
As I mentioned, 10 years have
now passed since the depths

01:55.816 --> 01:59.216 align:middle
of the financial crisis-a
painful part of our history

01:59.216 --> 02:02.416 align:middle
that cost many Americans
their jobs, their homes, and,

02:02.416 --> 02:03.906 align:middle
for some, their hopes
and dreams.

02:05.006 --> 02:06.826 align:middle
In addition to holding
interest rates low

02:06.826 --> 02:09.826 align:middle
to support the recovery, we
have also taken many steps

02:09.826 --> 02:11.396 align:middle
to make the financial
system safer.

02:12.136 --> 02:14.426 align:middle
In particular, we're
holding the largest banks

02:14.426 --> 02:16.646 align:middle
to much higher standards
in the amount of capital

02:16.676 --> 02:19.496 align:middle
and liquidity they hold
and in the ways they assess

02:19.556 --> 02:21.056 align:middle
and manage the risks they take.

02:21.786 --> 02:24.706 align:middle
I am confident that the
system today is stronger

02:24.966 --> 02:27.796 align:middle
and in a far better position
to support the financial needs

02:27.796 --> 02:31.256 align:middle
of households and businesses
through good times and bad.

02:32.586 --> 02:36.446 align:middle
We continue to work to sustain
these fundamental improvements

02:36.446 --> 02:37.446 align:middle
while also ensuring

02:37.446 --> 02:39.616 align:middle
that regulation is both
effective and efficient.

02:41.106 --> 02:43.366 align:middle
Now, let me go into more
detail about the developments

02:43.366 --> 02:45.456 align:middle
that motivated today's
policy decision.

02:47.246 --> 02:48.326 align:middle
Both household spending

02:48.326 --> 02:50.406 align:middle
and business investment
are expanding briskly,

02:50.456 --> 02:53.246 align:middle
and the overall growth
outlook remains favorable.

02:53.436 --> 02:55.446 align:middle
And several factors
support this assessment:

02:56.406 --> 02:59.386 align:middle
Fiscal policy is
boosting the economy,

02:59.446 --> 03:02.376 align:middle
ongoing job gains are raising
incomes and confidence,

03:02.916 --> 03:05.226 align:middle
and overall financial
conditions remain accommodative.

03:06.666 --> 03:09.456 align:middle
These conditions are
consistent with our Summary

03:09.456 --> 03:11.046 align:middle
of Economic Projections
for this meeting.

03:11.766 --> 03:13.876 align:middle
The median of Committee
participants' projections

03:13.876 --> 03:17.176 align:middle
for the growth of real GDP
is 3.1 percent this year

03:17.486 --> 03:19.146 align:middle
and 2.5 percent next year.

03:21.436 --> 03:24.316 align:middle
Job gains averaged
185,000 per month

03:24.356 --> 03:26.686 align:middle
over the last three months,
well above the pace needed

03:26.686 --> 03:28.366 align:middle
in the longer run
to provide jobs

03:28.366 --> 03:30.686 align:middle
for new entrants
to the labor force.

03:31.486 --> 03:34.506 align:middle
The unemployment rate stood
at 3.9 percent in August

03:34.506 --> 03:36.336 align:middle
and has been near that
level since April.

03:37.666 --> 03:39.656 align:middle
Smoothing through
month-to-month variations,

03:39.656 --> 03:42.416 align:middle
the labor force participation
rate remains about where it was

03:42.416 --> 03:46.316 align:middle
in late 2013-a positive
sign, given that the aging

03:46.316 --> 03:48.586 align:middle
of our population is
putting downward pressure

03:48.586 --> 03:49.386 align:middle
on participation.

03:50.336 --> 03:52.216 align:middle
We expect the job
market to remain strong.

03:52.636 --> 03:55.126 align:middle
And the median of Committee
participants' projections

03:55.126 --> 03:58.166 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
later this year is 3.7 percent

03:58.586 --> 04:01.726 align:middle
and a bit lower than
that in 2019.

04:03.526 --> 04:06.146 align:middle
Overall consumer prices, as
measured by the price index

04:06.146 --> 04:08.906 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures (PCE inflation),

04:09.186 --> 04:12.386 align:middle
increased 2.3 percent over
the 12 months ending in July.

04:13.276 --> 04:16.286 align:middle
The core price index, which
excludes the prices of energy

04:16.286 --> 04:18.226 align:middle
and food and tends to
be a better indicator

04:18.226 --> 04:21.526 align:middle
of future inflation, rose 2
percent over the same period.

04:22.936 --> 04:25.466 align:middle
The FOMC seeks, and
expects to see,

04:26.026 --> 04:29.076 align:middle
inflation remaining near our
2 percent longer-run objective

04:29.076 --> 04:30.456 align:middle
on a sustained basis.

04:31.186 --> 04:33.236 align:middle
At any given time,
inflation may be somewhat

04:33.236 --> 04:34.656 align:middle
above or below 2 percent.

04:35.076 --> 04:36.386 align:middle
For example, the recent rise

04:36.386 --> 04:38.856 align:middle
in oil prices has
pushed inflation a little

04:38.856 --> 04:41.726 align:middle
above 2 percent, but we
expect this to be transitory,

04:41.726 --> 04:43.386 align:middle
as is reflected in
our projections.

04:44.576 --> 04:46.486 align:middle
The median projections
for both the overall

04:46.486 --> 04:49.846 align:middle
and core inflation measures
remain very close to 2 percent

04:49.846 --> 04:51.826 align:middle
across the whole
projection horizon.

04:53.596 --> 04:55.906 align:middle
Today the Committee
raised the target range

04:55.906 --> 04:57.966 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
by ¼ percentage point,

04:58.396 --> 05:00.486 align:middle
bringing it to 2 to 2¼ percent.

05:01.516 --> 05:04.066 align:middle
This action reflects the
strength we see in the economy

05:04.066 --> 05:05.826 align:middle
and is one more step
in the process

05:05.856 --> 05:07.686 align:middle
that we began almost
three years ago

05:08.206 --> 05:11.276 align:middle
of gradually returning interest
rates to more normal levels.

05:12.826 --> 05:15.686 align:middle
Looking ahead, today's
projections show gradual

05:15.746 --> 05:17.786 align:middle
interest rate increases
continuing roughly

05:17.786 --> 05:19.166 align:middle
as foreseen in June.

05:20.066 --> 05:22.636 align:middle
The median of participants'
views on appropriate policy

05:22.636 --> 05:25.726 align:middle
through 2020 are unchanged
since the June meeting.

05:27.276 --> 05:29.536 align:middle
These projections play an
important role in our work.

05:29.886 --> 05:31.496 align:middle
My colleagues and I
believe that it's useful

05:31.496 --> 05:34.036 align:middle
to communicate our views
on likely prospects

05:34.036 --> 05:35.476 align:middle
for the economy and for policy.

05:36.326 --> 05:39.486 align:middle
We are well aware, however, that
the economy regularly surprises,

05:39.486 --> 05:43.286 align:middle
and that even the most carefully
constructed projections are

05:43.286 --> 05:44.136 align:middle
highly uncertain.

05:45.236 --> 05:46.856 align:middle
The projections about
the appropriate path

05:46.856 --> 05:49.976 align:middle
of policy assume that the
economy evolves broadly in line

05:49.976 --> 05:51.886 align:middle
with the projections
for growth, employment,

05:51.886 --> 05:53.116 align:middle
unemployment, and inflation.

05:54.306 --> 05:56.126 align:middle
If the economy were
instead to falter,

05:56.946 --> 05:58.486 align:middle
lower interest rates
would be appropriate.

05:58.896 --> 06:01.576 align:middle
Conversely, if inflationary
pressures were to rise more

06:01.576 --> 06:04.146 align:middle
than expected, higher interest
rates would be appropriate.

06:04.696 --> 06:06.596 align:middle
Right now, as our
statement indicates,

06:06.906 --> 06:10.786 align:middle
risks to the economic outlook
appear roughly balanced.

06:12.436 --> 06:14.976 align:middle
Readers of the FOMC
statement likely noted

06:15.116 --> 06:17.226 align:middle
that the Committee dropped a
sentence that indicated that

06:17.226 --> 06:19.546 align:middle
"the stance of monetary
policy remains accommodative."

06:20.686 --> 06:24.366 align:middle
This change does not signal
any change in the likely path

06:24.366 --> 06:28.116 align:middle
of policy; instead, it is a
sign that policy is proceeding

06:28.116 --> 06:29.666 align:middle
in line with our expectations.

06:30.366 --> 06:32.476 align:middle
We still expect, as
our statement says,

06:32.966 --> 06:35.116 align:middle
"further gradual increases
in the target range

06:35.116 --> 06:38.106 align:middle
for the federal funds rate," and
this expectation is reflected

06:38.136 --> 06:39.716 align:middle
in the projections
that I just discussed.

06:40.366 --> 06:41.196 align:middle
Thanks very much.

06:41.436 --> 06:42.876 align:middle
I'm happy to take
your questions.

06:44.036 --> 06:45.806 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

06:48.676 --> 06:49.436 align:middle
Thank you.

06:49.596 --> 06:51.496 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

06:52.236 --> 06:56.116 align:middle
Chair Powell, given the
lags of monetary policy,

06:56.186 --> 06:57.546 align:middle
I want to know how you think

06:57.546 --> 07:00.226 align:middle
about ending the
tightening cycle?

07:00.766 --> 07:02.906 align:middle
How will you know when to stop?

07:03.396 --> 07:05.926 align:middle
And do you need to keep
going until something

07:05.926 --> 07:06.866 align:middle
in the economy breaks?

07:07.596 --> 07:09.006 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

07:10.256 --> 07:13.456 align:middle
So the tightening cycle, as
you know, is a reflection

07:13.456 --> 07:14.676 align:middle
of the strength of the economy.

07:14.676 --> 07:16.926 align:middle
And it's almost three years now

07:16.926 --> 07:18.576 align:middle
that we've been gradually
raising rates.

07:18.576 --> 07:21.046 align:middle
And I think the fact that we
have moved quite gradually,

07:21.836 --> 07:27.486 align:middle
in a way, allows us to
carefully watch incoming data

07:27.596 --> 07:29.626 align:middle
in the real economy and
in the financial markets

07:31.066 --> 07:33.186 align:middle
to see how the economy
is processing higher

07:33.186 --> 07:33.796 align:middle
interest rates.

07:34.436 --> 07:35.736 align:middle
And the fact that we're moving

07:35.736 --> 07:39.336 align:middle
so gradually-I think it-I
think it limits the long

07:39.336 --> 07:41.826 align:middle
and variable lags
problem because, you know,

07:42.016 --> 07:44.736 align:middle
we're being able to
raise rates and then wait

07:44.736 --> 07:48.066 align:middle
and see how the economy
absorbs these rate increases.

07:48.066 --> 07:50.566 align:middle
And so far, the economy
has performed very well

07:50.566 --> 07:52.746 align:middle
and very much in keeping
with our expectations.

07:53.236 --> 07:56.096 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

07:56.646 --> 08:02.266 align:middle
Hi, Jim Puzzanghera
from the L.A. Times.

08:03.186 --> 08:05.626 align:middle
President Trump has stated
publicly he's not thrilled

08:05.766 --> 08:06.726 align:middle
with the interest rate hikes.

08:07.276 --> 08:09.056 align:middle
His comments didn't
appear to have any impact

08:09.056 --> 08:10.176 align:middle
on you or your colleagues.

08:10.656 --> 08:12.316 align:middle
Were his comments
discussed at your meeting?

08:12.676 --> 08:15.066 align:middle
And do you have any concerns
about the effect of these types

08:15.116 --> 08:17.866 align:middle
of comments on the perception
of the Fed's independence

08:18.156 --> 08:19.126 align:middle
and your monetary policy?

08:19.126 --> 08:20.056 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

08:20.406 --> 08:24.506 align:middle
So we've been given a really
important job to do on behalf

08:24.506 --> 08:26.436 align:middle
of the American people
by Congress,

08:26.616 --> 08:29.996 align:middle
and we've been given the tools
to do it, and my colleagues

08:29.996 --> 08:33.316 align:middle
and I are focused exclusively
on carrying out that mission.

08:33.986 --> 08:36.286 align:middle
We consider the best
thinking, the best theory,

08:36.286 --> 08:37.336 align:middle
and the best evidence.

08:37.786 --> 08:39.336 align:middle
We have disparate
points of view,

08:39.336 --> 08:43.086 align:middle
which we debate extensively and
come to a perspective and try

08:43.086 --> 08:46.036 align:middle
to set monetary policy to
achieve maximum employment

08:46.906 --> 08:48.286 align:middle
in a context of price stability.

08:48.346 --> 08:48.986 align:middle
That's what we do.

08:48.986 --> 08:53.276 align:middle
We don't consider political
factors or things like that.

08:53.276 --> 08:57.256 align:middle
And so that's who we
are, that's what we do,

08:57.256 --> 08:59.776 align:middle
and that's just the way it's
always going to be for us.

09:00.266 --> 09:02.296 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:04.326 --> 09:05.096 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

09:05.096 --> 09:06.586 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

09:07.226 --> 09:12.946 align:middle
Can you explain how-the
stock market being

09:13.016 --> 09:14.146 align:middle
at a near all-time high,

09:14.146 --> 09:18.216 align:middle
corporate spreads being very
tight-how does the level

09:18.216 --> 09:20.496 align:middle
of the market factor
into your decisions

09:20.496 --> 09:22.266 align:middle
to raise interest rates?

09:22.266 --> 09:23.616 align:middle
Is there a concern on your part?

09:23.616 --> 09:26.086 align:middle
And then, as sort of a
second and related part,

09:26.086 --> 09:28.886 align:middle
can you tell us what
happens in 2020

09:28.886 --> 09:32.566 align:middle
and 2021 that-I know
you can't tell us

09:32.566 --> 09:36.206 align:middle
when you're shaking your head
[laughter]-that prompts-that

09:36.206 --> 09:39.536 align:middle
prompts you-the Fed to think
that the rates need to still be

09:39.536 --> 09:41.496 align:middle
above neutral in
that time period?

09:41.906 --> 09:42.256 align:middle
Thank you.

09:42.536 --> 09:43.006 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

09:43.006 --> 09:46.586 align:middle
Sure. So in terms of financial
markets and monetary policy,

09:46.586 --> 09:50.046 align:middle
we-as we say in our
statement every cycle,

09:50.046 --> 09:53.796 align:middle
we do take financial
conditions into consideration

09:54.516 --> 09:56.716 align:middle
because financial-broader
financial conditions do affect

09:56.716 --> 09:59.426 align:middle
the broader economy, and
they're one of the many things

09:59.426 --> 10:00.406 align:middle
that we take into account.

10:00.996 --> 10:03.476 align:middle
The part of it that we
control, though, is what we do

10:03.476 --> 10:06.386 align:middle
with the federal funds rate
and that has-that has effects

10:06.506 --> 10:07.686 align:middle
out through, you know,

10:07.686 --> 10:09.646 align:middle
out through the full
interest rate curve

10:09.646 --> 10:11.456 align:middle
and into financial
asset prices generally.

10:11.456 --> 10:14.866 align:middle
So the answer is, we take
everything into account, but,

10:15.356 --> 10:17.236 align:middle
you know, broader financial
conditions are just one

10:17.236 --> 10:19.936 align:middle
of the things that
we take into account.

10:19.936 --> 10:21.526 align:middle
In terms of '20 and '21,

10:21.526 --> 10:24.166 align:middle
why-your question was
why the funds rate needs

10:24.166 --> 10:25.616 align:middle
to be above neutral?

10:26.466 --> 10:27.736 align:middle
So you're right.

10:27.736 --> 10:32.506 align:middle
Some of-some of the participants
have mostly very modest

10:32.506 --> 10:34.706 align:middle
overshoots of their
personal estimates

10:34.706 --> 10:37.836 align:middle
of neutral a couple-three
years out.

10:37.986 --> 10:42.226 align:middle
You know, it's far
out into the future.

10:42.226 --> 10:44.476 align:middle
I think it's hard
to be confident

10:44.476 --> 10:46.806 align:middle
that that's-that that's the
way things are going to be.

10:46.806 --> 10:48.636 align:middle
What we're going to be
doing as we-as we go

10:48.636 --> 10:52.606 align:middle
through time is asking at every
meeting whether monetary policy

10:52.606 --> 10:54.696 align:middle
is set to achieve our goals.

10:55.266 --> 10:57.116 align:middle
And I think that
that's an assessment

10:57.116 --> 11:01.306 align:middle
of where policy will need to
be some years down the road,

11:01.666 --> 11:08.886 align:middle
and I'll leave it at that.

11:08.966 --> 11:09.996 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

11:09.996 --> 11:11.626 align:middle
Thanks. Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

11:11.626 --> 11:14.396 align:middle
Chair Powell, I think-is
this too good to be true?

11:14.616 --> 11:16.506 align:middle
Because you've got
four years here

11:16.506 --> 11:19.286 align:middle
of unemployment below
4 percent-very low,

11:19.876 --> 11:22.386 align:middle
substantially below
NAIRU, what appears

11:22.386 --> 11:26.306 align:middle
to be NAIRU-but no
reaction of inflation there.

11:26.926 --> 11:30.706 align:middle
And so I don't think that's
ever happened since the 1960s.

11:30.706 --> 11:32.266 align:middle
Why do you think it's
going to happen now?

11:33.456 --> 11:35.396 align:middle
Or in the alternative,
is this forecast

11:35.396 --> 11:36.546 align:middle
for unemployment
just being driven

11:36.546 --> 11:39.146 align:middle
by your confidence intervals
to the extent that, well,

11:39.346 --> 11:42.636 align:middle
we don't know what else to do,
so just extend the lie out-line

11:42.636 --> 11:43.626 align:middle
out until something happens?

11:44.106 --> 11:44.946 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

11:45.526 --> 11:46.266 align:middle
No, you're right.

11:46.266 --> 11:50.186 align:middle
The Committee forecasts what
they-what they show, in general,

11:50.786 --> 11:54.866 align:middle
is an economy where
unemployment remains in the high

11:54.866 --> 11:57.386 align:middle
and middle 3s throughout
the entire forecast period

11:58.496 --> 12:01.746 align:middle
and inflation remains
very close to 2 percent.

12:01.996 --> 12:03.236 align:middle
That is what the forecast shows.

12:04.066 --> 12:06.926 align:middle
And that is based
on our understanding

12:06.926 --> 12:10.076 align:middle
of the way the inflation process
works now and on the fact

12:10.156 --> 12:14.686 align:middle
that the inflation seems to be
fairly nonreactive to changes

12:14.686 --> 12:16.966 align:middle
in slack-that is to say,
a flat Phillips curve.

12:16.966 --> 12:20.086 align:middle
And it's just-it's
a-it's a world

12:20.246 --> 12:22.566 align:middle
of strongly anchored
inflation expectations.

12:22.946 --> 12:25.826 align:middle
And that's not just our
forecast, that's many forecasts.

12:25.826 --> 12:28.466 align:middle
So that is-that is
-that is the forecast.

12:28.466 --> 12:30.496 align:middle
So you may ask-I
mean, the sense is,

12:31.026 --> 12:33.736 align:middle
what does the longer-run natural
rate of unemployment mean?

12:33.736 --> 12:36.136 align:middle
And so that really is
a longer-run concept

12:36.746 --> 12:40.256 align:middle
that we think the economy will
return to over the longer run.

12:41.016 --> 12:43.686 align:middle
But, obviously, that
longer run, you know,

12:43.686 --> 12:45.496 align:middle
isn't-it's not creating problems

12:45.496 --> 12:47.276 align:middle
in the short run
for that forecast.

12:47.706 --> 12:51.416 align:middle
Now let's just admit that the
inflation process has changed

12:51.416 --> 12:54.886 align:middle
dramatically from where it was
in the 1960s to where it is now.

12:54.886 --> 12:56.696 align:middle
It's in a good place now.

12:57.586 --> 12:59.686 align:middle
We can't take for granted that
it will remain the way it is,

12:59.686 --> 13:02.176 align:middle
and, you know, we will be, of
course, watching carefully.

13:02.286 --> 13:04.946 align:middle
If inflation reacts more
strongly or more weakly

13:05.566 --> 13:06.536 align:middle
to the strong economy,

13:06.536 --> 13:08.266 align:middle
then we'll adjust
policy appropriately.

13:08.576 --> 13:08.886 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:08.886 --> 13:10.096 align:middle
If I could follow up on that,

13:10.096 --> 13:11.326 align:middle
what are you buying
with interest rates?

13:14.806 --> 13:15.316 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

13:15.506 --> 13:19.226 align:middle
Well, interest rates, of
course, have effects on growth,

13:19.226 --> 13:23.246 align:middle
have effects-I mean if-I
guess the-your question was,

13:23.246 --> 13:25.126 align:middle
what are you buying with
by raising interest rates?

13:25.126 --> 13:26.316 align:middle
The question is, what
would the economy look

13:26.316 --> 13:27.446 align:middle
like if you didn't raise rates?

13:27.646 --> 13:29.566 align:middle
It would look very different, I
think, if we didn't raise rates.

13:29.856 --> 13:33.076 align:middle
We're always trying to,
you know, work our way

13:33.076 --> 13:37.266 align:middle
between the-sort of the
two-the two problems we face.

13:37.266 --> 13:40.396 align:middle
One is that if we move too
quickly, we can, you know,

13:40.396 --> 13:43.276 align:middle
snuff out a recovery
unnecessarily,

13:43.456 --> 13:45.666 align:middle
and inflation falls short
of its 2 percent target.

13:45.666 --> 13:47.026 align:middle
Or if we move too slowly,

13:47.026 --> 13:48.526 align:middle
we have an economy
that can overheat.

13:48.526 --> 13:50.896 align:middle
So we're-that-that's
happened through history.

13:50.896 --> 13:53.186 align:middle
We don't see any signs of that
now, but we're always trying

13:53.186 --> 13:55.706 align:middle
to navigate between
those two shoals.

13:56.196 --> 13:58.376 align:middle
And we think that
gradually, you know,

13:58.686 --> 14:01.816 align:middle
raising interest rates is the
way that we kind of take both

14:01.816 --> 14:03.796 align:middle
of those risks seriously.

14:05.056 --> 14:06.466 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

14:08.336 --> 14:09.546 align:middle
Thanks very much.

14:09.546 --> 14:10.966 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

14:11.226 --> 14:12.716 align:middle
The Fed has recently been saying

14:12.716 --> 14:15.816 align:middle
that financial stability
risks are moderate.

14:16.316 --> 14:20.166 align:middle
What kind of developments
could prompt a change

14:20.166 --> 14:22.986 align:middle
in that assessment
to elevated risks?

14:23.046 --> 14:25.106 align:middle
And is there an argument

14:25.106 --> 14:27.716 align:middle
for moving the countercyclical
capital buffer,

14:28.016 --> 14:30.656 align:middle
even when risks are
moderate, rather than waiting

14:30.656 --> 14:32.896 align:middle
until the risks are
seen as elevated, which,

14:32.896 --> 14:34.506 align:middle
arguably, could be too late?

14:34.886 --> 14:35.866 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

14:35.866 --> 14:38.456 align:middle
So I think one of the
most important lessons

14:38.456 --> 14:40.646 align:middle
from the financial crisis
is really the importance

14:41.346 --> 14:43.046 align:middle
of the stability of
the financial system.

14:43.196 --> 14:47.236 align:middle
And so we-since the financial
crisis, as you're aware,

14:47.456 --> 14:51.496 align:middle
we've had a major focus on
building up our monitoring

14:51.496 --> 14:54.196 align:middle
of financial conditions and
financial stability issues.

14:54.426 --> 14:57.466 align:middle
We've also put in place
many, many initiatives

14:57.466 --> 14:59.856 align:middle
to strengthen the financial
system through higher capital

14:59.856 --> 15:02.886 align:middle
and better regulation, more
transparency, central clearing,

15:02.886 --> 15:05.986 align:middle
margins on uncleared
derivatives, all kinds of things

15:05.986 --> 15:08.306 align:middle
like that, which are meant to
strengthen the financial system.

15:08.356 --> 15:10.256 align:middle
We've done many of those things,

15:10.256 --> 15:11.956 align:middle
and we feel that the
financial system is

15:11.956 --> 15:13.816 align:middle
in a much better place.

15:14.256 --> 15:17.326 align:middle
As we look at-as we look

15:17.326 --> 15:20.106 align:middle
at conditions most recently-and
this was at our last meeting,

15:20.106 --> 15:22.996 align:middle
and it was in our minutes for
the last meeting-quarterly,

15:22.996 --> 15:25.696 align:middle
we have a briefing at the
FOMC, and also at the Board,

15:26.116 --> 15:28.626 align:middle
of the staffs' views
on financial stability,

15:28.626 --> 15:32.686 align:middle
and the staff judged the overall
vulnerabilities to be moderate.

15:32.806 --> 15:36.406 align:middle
If you dig into the different
aspects of that question,

15:36.776 --> 15:39.456 align:middle
households or balance
sheets are in good shape.

15:39.456 --> 15:41.166 align:middle
You know, employment is high,

15:41.166 --> 15:43.426 align:middle
wages are rising,
that sort of thing.

15:43.676 --> 15:46.976 align:middle
The banking system, as I
mentioned, is-you know,

15:47.166 --> 15:50.656 align:middle
much higher capital, much higher
liquidity-is much stronger.

15:51.176 --> 15:52.556 align:middle
If you look at asset prices,

15:52.556 --> 15:56.386 align:middle
it is true that some asset
prices are in the upper range

15:56.386 --> 15:59.966 align:middle
of their historical-upper reach
of their historical ranges.

16:00.596 --> 16:03.706 align:middle
And then if you look at
nonfinancial corporates,

16:03.706 --> 16:06.076 align:middle
you get-there is the
story of leverage there.

16:06.076 --> 16:08.336 align:middle
So it's not that there
aren't any vulnerabilities,

16:08.376 --> 16:09.716 align:middle
but we see them as moderate.

16:09.716 --> 16:12.276 align:middle
Now, you ask about the
countercyclical capital buffer.

16:12.276 --> 16:15.786 align:middle
The countercyclical capital
buffer is a tool that we added

16:15.786 --> 16:19.516 align:middle
to our toolkit, I think,
finally, back in 2016.

16:19.956 --> 16:21.726 align:middle
We said that we would deploy it

16:21.976 --> 16:24.516 align:middle
when vulnerabilities were
meaningfully above normal.

16:25.116 --> 16:27.446 align:middle
We revisit that on
a regular schedule,

16:27.596 --> 16:29.586 align:middle
and the last time we
revisited it was, I think,

16:30.106 --> 16:32.546 align:middle
last year-sometime
last year-and concluded

16:32.546 --> 16:35.276 align:middle
that the test was not met.

16:35.816 --> 16:39.576 align:middle
As I mentioned at the last
briefing, the-you know,

16:40.136 --> 16:42.386 align:middle
the assessment was that
vulnerabilities were moderate,

16:42.386 --> 16:46.096 align:middle
but it remains a kit-a tool for
our toolkit that we can deploy,

16:46.656 --> 16:48.446 align:middle
you know, when and as
we feel appropriate.

16:48.956 --> 16:49.386 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

16:49.466 --> 16:51.306 align:middle
Has the chance of needing
to deploy it increased?

16:53.766 --> 16:54.626 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

16:54.626 --> 16:58.086 align:middle
As I said, I think I'm in the
camp of seeing vulnerabilities

16:58.086 --> 16:59.246 align:middle
as relatively moderate.

16:59.436 --> 17:02.846 align:middle
I think the financial
system-I think another thing

17:02.846 --> 17:05.366 align:middle
that we learned in the crisis
is that we need to be alert

17:05.366 --> 17:07.576 align:middle
to the buildup of
vulnerabilities

17:07.576 --> 17:10.936 align:middle
as a long expansion continues.

17:11.536 --> 17:14.586 align:middle
I don't see that in
troubling-troubling amounts

17:14.586 --> 17:16.906 align:middle
at this point, but, you know,
I think it's appropriate for us

17:17.066 --> 17:18.406 align:middle
to monitor that very carefully.

17:19.516 --> 17:22.286 align:middle
MARTY CRUTSINGER.

17:22.286 --> 17:24.766 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

17:24.766 --> 17:27.286 align:middle
The researchers at the European
Central Bank this week put

17:27.286 --> 17:32.846 align:middle
out a paper saying that
the impact of a trade war

17:32.846 --> 17:38.266 align:middle
on the U.S. could be dire, that
the U.S. economy could drop

17:38.266 --> 17:42.826 align:middle
by 2 percent in the first year
after a widespread trade war.

17:43.326 --> 17:47.406 align:middle
Could you talk about the
discussion you've had-your

17:47.406 --> 17:51.056 align:middle
policy statement, does it
mention the trade tensions?

17:51.086 --> 17:52.446 align:middle
You did not mention them.

17:52.706 --> 17:55.606 align:middle
What do you-what kind of
outlook do you have right now

17:56.016 --> 17:57.246 align:middle
for the trade war?

17:57.246 --> 17:57.546 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

17:57.686 --> 17:59.786 align:middle
Sure. So I guess I
need to start by saying

17:59.786 --> 18:01.796 align:middle
that we're not responsible
for trade policy,

18:01.796 --> 18:04.306 align:middle
and we don't comment on
particular, you know,

18:04.346 --> 18:07.886 align:middle
trade actions and
that sort of thing.

18:07.886 --> 18:13.606 align:middle
But you will have seen that we
have this very extensive network

18:13.606 --> 18:15.246 align:middle
of business contacts
around the country

18:15.246 --> 18:16.986 align:middle
through the Reserve
Banks largely,

18:16.986 --> 18:19.976 align:middle
and we've been hearing a
rising chorus of concerns

18:20.336 --> 18:23.866 align:middle
from businesses all over
the country about disruption

18:23.866 --> 18:26.476 align:middle
of supply chains, materials
costs and increases,

18:27.156 --> 18:28.916 align:middle
and loss of markets,
and things like that.

18:28.916 --> 18:30.556 align:middle
So we've been hearing
quite a lot of that,

18:30.676 --> 18:33.976 align:middle
and we-the Reserve Bank
presidents talk about it

18:33.976 --> 18:35.216 align:middle
in the meeting, and it
goes in the minutes.

18:35.216 --> 18:36.806 align:middle
So we, you know,
that's-we're very transparent

18:36.806 --> 18:37.566 align:middle
about hearing all that.

18:38.336 --> 18:40.006 align:middle
I think if you look

18:40.056 --> 18:43.736 align:middle
at the aggregate perform-the
performance of the U.S. economy

18:43.736 --> 18:45.436 align:middle
at an aggregate-at
a national level,

18:45.906 --> 18:48.426 align:middle
it's hard to see much
happening at this point.

18:48.926 --> 18:51.216 align:middle
And-or you can look at it
the other way: You can ask,

18:51.436 --> 18:54.986 align:middle
if all of the tariffs that have
been announced are applied,

18:54.986 --> 18:56.756 align:middle
what would be the effect
at the aggregate level?

18:56.956 --> 18:58.916 align:middle
And they're still
relatively small.

18:59.186 --> 19:01.416 align:middle
You know, we worry about
a couple of things.

19:01.416 --> 19:03.276 align:middle
One is loss of business
confidence,

19:03.276 --> 19:04.416 align:middle
which could reduce investment.

19:04.676 --> 19:06.086 align:middle
Again, we don't really
see effects

19:06.086 --> 19:07.276 align:middle
that we could measure yet.

19:07.776 --> 19:10.086 align:middle
Also, the possibility of a
financial market reaction

19:10.246 --> 19:13.356 align:middle
over time to unexpected
trade developments.

19:14.016 --> 19:17.936 align:middle
More than anything, though, I
would worry, in the longer run,

19:17.936 --> 19:19.006 align:middle
where this is going, you know.

19:19.006 --> 19:22.726 align:middle
If the-if the end place we
get to is lower tariffs,

19:22.726 --> 19:24.166 align:middle
that would be good, you know.

19:24.166 --> 19:26.336 align:middle
Trade generally supports
productivity,

19:26.336 --> 19:27.566 align:middle
supports higher incomes.

19:28.106 --> 19:30.766 align:middle
And, you know, fair
trade-fair trade

19:30.766 --> 19:34.566 align:middle
under internationally accepted
rules can be a good thing.

19:34.696 --> 19:38.276 align:middle
I think if this, perhaps
inadvertently, goes to a place

19:38.276 --> 19:41.596 align:middle
where we have widespread
tariffs that remain in place

19:41.596 --> 19:44.996 align:middle
for a long time-a more
protectionist world-that's going

19:44.996 --> 19:46.986 align:middle
to be bad for the
United States' economy

19:46.986 --> 19:48.826 align:middle
and for American
workers, and families,

19:48.906 --> 19:50.366 align:middle
and also for other economies.

19:52.276 --> 19:54.596 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

19:55.006 --> 19:57.346 align:middle
Hi, Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

19:58.136 --> 19:59.656 align:middle
This is the third time this year

19:59.656 --> 20:02.576 align:middle
that the FOMC has raised
its growth forecasts.

20:02.976 --> 20:04.616 align:middle
I'm wondering if you
could share a little bit

20:04.616 --> 20:07.036 align:middle
about what's causing that rise

20:07.036 --> 20:09.976 align:middle
and whether you see
supply-side effects coming

20:09.976 --> 20:13.176 align:middle
through from the fiscal
policy-fiscal stimulus yet.

20:13.356 --> 20:14.116 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

20:14.336 --> 20:15.066 align:middle
So it's true.

20:16.166 --> 20:19.796 align:middle
This year has-we've kept raising
our forecast for this year.

20:19.796 --> 20:22.946 align:middle
This-as the year's gone on, the
economy has come in stronger

20:22.946 --> 20:24.826 align:middle
than we expected, and
that's a really good thing.

20:24.996 --> 20:28.926 align:middle
And the economy, as I mentioned
at the beginning, is strong,

20:28.926 --> 20:30.636 align:middle
and there's good reason to
expect that to continue.

20:31.146 --> 20:32.076 align:middle
You ask why.

20:32.076 --> 20:34.486 align:middle
You know, some of
it is, no doubt,

20:34.596 --> 20:37.756 align:middle
the effect of the fiscal
policy changes, the tax cuts,

20:37.756 --> 20:39.316 align:middle
and the spending
increases-that's got

20:39.316 --> 20:40.206 align:middle
to be part of the story.

20:40.916 --> 20:44.096 align:middle
Part of it may be higher oil
prices, which are calling

20:44.096 --> 20:46.366 align:middle
for more investment
in the oil patch.

20:46.956 --> 20:50.166 align:middle
But, you know, the growth
picture is very much supported

20:50.166 --> 20:52.676 align:middle
by very high readings
of household confidence,

20:52.716 --> 20:56.206 align:middle
business confidence, so it's
a-it's a-it's a-it's a pretty

20:56.206 --> 20:58.206 align:middle
particularly bright
moment, I think.

20:58.206 --> 21:00.236 align:middle
If you look back
over the last decade,

21:00.236 --> 21:03.046 align:middle
this is a pretty good
moment for the U.S. economy.

21:03.936 --> 21:04.166 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

21:04.166 --> 21:04.916 align:middle
And the supply-side effects?

21:04.916 --> 21:05.316 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

21:05.346 --> 21:06.406 align:middle
Oh, supply-side effects, yeah.

21:06.726 --> 21:09.376 align:middle
So, you know, we hope
they're huge, frankly.

21:09.476 --> 21:13.696 align:middle
You know, the idea is, you
cut-you reduce taxation

21:13.696 --> 21:14.706 align:middle
on corporations

21:14.706 --> 21:17.146 align:middle
and particularly-and allow
faster expensing for investment.

21:17.946 --> 21:21.706 align:middle
The idea is to encourage more
investment, and that is one

21:21.706 --> 21:23.606 align:middle
of the things that drives
productivity, which is one

21:23.606 --> 21:25.406 align:middle
of the main things that
drives rising incomes.

21:25.956 --> 21:29.026 align:middle
So the effects are very
uncertain, they're-you know,

21:29.026 --> 21:32.596 align:middle
they're-the literature
is-it's-there's no clear answer

21:32.596 --> 21:35.416 align:middle
to exactly how these mechanisms
work or how effective they are.

21:35.836 --> 21:38.136 align:middle
And it would be soon to be
seeing supply-side effects.

21:38.136 --> 21:41.786 align:middle
The other-supply-side effects
are probably both more uncertain

21:41.786 --> 21:45.816 align:middle
and they're probably
slower to come in.

21:45.876 --> 21:49.186 align:middle
But, you know, as I said,
we-I certainly hope-I hope

21:49.256 --> 21:52.106 align:middle
that we'll be marking
up our estimates

21:52.106 --> 21:53.116 align:middle
of potential growth as well.

21:53.596 --> 21:55.126 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

21:56.806 --> 21:58.716 align:middle
Craig Torres from
Bloomberg News.

21:59.246 --> 22:01.836 align:middle
Taking a page from your
risk-management approach

22:01.836 --> 22:05.526 align:middle
in Jackson Hole, Mr. Chairman,
I'm wondering if you can talk

22:05.526 --> 22:09.846 align:middle
about what risks would make the
Committee-plausible risks-go

22:09.846 --> 22:11.476 align:middle
faster in 2019.

22:12.366 --> 22:14.856 align:middle
What risks would
make them go slower?

22:15.046 --> 22:16.596 align:middle
If you could talk
about scenarios.

22:16.716 --> 22:20.946 align:middle
And then I'd like to
ask, do you see yourself,

22:20.946 --> 22:24.656 align:middle
as this stimulus tails off,
which it does in the forecast,

22:24.836 --> 22:28.486 align:middle
possibly cutting rates
to soft-land the economy?

22:29.236 --> 22:31.446 align:middle
I know the forecast
doesn't show that,

22:31.476 --> 22:34.516 align:middle
but I'm curious how you
soft-land without doing that.

22:35.456 --> 22:35.806 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

22:35.806 --> 22:37.896 align:middle
Okay, so I think I mentioned

22:37.896 --> 22:39.956 align:middle
in the opening remarks
maybe something

22:39.956 --> 22:40.946 align:middle
that addressed your question

22:40.946 --> 22:42.776 align:middle
about what would make
us go faster and slower.

22:43.296 --> 22:47.316 align:middle
You know, the main thing
where we might need

22:47.316 --> 22:50.106 align:middle
to move along a little
bit quicker would be

22:50.416 --> 22:52.686 align:middle
if inflation-inflation
surprises to the upside.

22:53.806 --> 22:54.516 align:middle
We don't see that.

22:54.766 --> 22:55.656 align:middle
We really don't see that.

22:55.846 --> 22:57.316 align:middle
It's not in our forecast,

22:57.466 --> 23:02.196 align:middle
but any-so I would-I
regard that as a risk.

23:02.196 --> 23:08.716 align:middle
I think either a significant
significant correction

23:08.716 --> 23:11.696 align:middle
and lasting correction in
financial markets or a slowing

23:11.696 --> 23:14.256 align:middle
down in the economy
that's inconsistent

23:14.256 --> 23:18.356 align:middle
with our forecast-those are the
kinds of things we'd react to.

23:18.636 --> 23:22.186 align:middle
In terms of what, you know,
what we would do-you know,

23:22.186 --> 23:23.976 align:middle
I think we're always going

23:23.976 --> 23:27.246 align:middle
to be adjusting monetary
policy to-in light

23:27.246 --> 23:28.466 align:middle
of conditions on the ground.

23:28.466 --> 23:29.796 align:middle
So if the economy is weakening,

23:30.176 --> 23:34.646 align:middle
then it's very possible we'd
be-we'd be cutting rates and,

23:35.046 --> 23:35.816 align:middle
you know, vice versa.

23:36.106 --> 23:37.426 align:middle
And so I can't rule that out.

23:37.426 --> 23:39.686 align:middle
It-I think it's quite
difficult, though,

23:39.686 --> 23:43.926 align:middle
to know what the effects-what
the sort of end effects

23:43.926 --> 23:46.296 align:middle
of fiscal policy are
going to be in 2020.

23:46.816 --> 23:48.956 align:middle
It's difficult to know
what the policy will be,

23:48.956 --> 23:50.776 align:middle
let alone what their
effects will be that far out.

23:50.776 --> 23:52.286 align:middle
So we have to make estimates,

23:52.286 --> 23:54.286 align:middle
but we know that
they're highly uncertain.

23:55.546 --> 23:57.446 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

23:57.446 --> 23:58.746 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

23:58.746 --> 23:59.456 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman.

23:59.856 --> 24:01.956 align:middle
The unemployment rate
is now at 3.9 percent.

24:01.986 --> 24:05.236 align:middle
The average hourly
wages rose 2.9 percent

24:05.236 --> 24:06.126 align:middle
for the past 12 months.

24:06.396 --> 24:08.806 align:middle
Inflation, according to your
numbers, holding steady.

24:08.806 --> 24:12.326 align:middle
The amount of job vacancies that
we have out there and the number

24:12.326 --> 24:15.866 align:middle
of people coming back into the
workforce this year-where is the

24:15.866 --> 24:18.636 align:middle
point at which, then, we are
actually above the natural rate

24:18.676 --> 24:22.046 align:middle
of unemployment and the Federal
Reserve should reexamine the

24:22.046 --> 24:24.506 align:middle
consistency of the rate
hikes going forward so not

24:24.506 --> 24:26.036 align:middle
to cool an expanding economy?

24:26.456 --> 24:27.376 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

24:27.556 --> 24:28.996 align:middle
You mean-you mean,
where's the point

24:28.996 --> 24:31.186 align:middle
at which inflation-unemployment
is too low?

24:31.456 --> 24:31.946 align:middle
Yeah. Yeah.

24:32.046 --> 24:35.226 align:middle
You know, so we've been-I
think the perform-labor force

24:35.226 --> 24:37.556 align:middle
participation has
surprised us on the upside.

24:38.446 --> 24:40.026 align:middle
There is a long-run trend there,

24:40.166 --> 24:44.246 align:middle
and we've now had
basically five flat years.

24:44.246 --> 24:46.116 align:middle
If you look-if you look at
labor force participation,

24:46.116 --> 24:50.126 align:middle
since the fourth quarter of '13
till now, you really have a-kind

24:50.126 --> 24:53.506 align:middle
of an oscillation that
basically is a sideways movement

24:53.896 --> 24:56.826 align:middle
in the face of a
significant downward trend,

24:56.826 --> 24:58.526 align:middle
thanks to aging and
other factors.

24:59.036 --> 25:00.866 align:middle
That's a gain of well more
than a percentage point

25:00.866 --> 25:04.006 align:middle
in participation, so that's
really-that's really a

25:04.006 --> 25:04.486 align:middle
good thing.

25:04.486 --> 25:06.646 align:middle
Those are people who
are remaining in contact

25:06.646 --> 25:09.306 align:middle
with the labor force and
looking for jobs, finding jobs.

25:09.306 --> 25:12.426 align:middle
So that's a great thing.

25:12.426 --> 25:13.986 align:middle
And, by the way, it's a signal.

25:14.576 --> 25:17.896 align:middle
It's one of the things that has
allowed the unemployment rate

25:17.896 --> 25:18.846 align:middle
not to fall further.

25:18.846 --> 25:22.386 align:middle
And it's a-it's a signal, too,
that there may be more there

25:22.386 --> 25:25.066 align:middle
on the supply side, that
there may be more labor supply

25:25.666 --> 25:27.986 align:middle
than the prior trend
would indicate.

25:28.196 --> 25:29.906 align:middle
So it's a very positive thing

25:30.526 --> 25:32.486 align:middle
to see people staying
in the labor force.

25:32.566 --> 25:34.716 align:middle
And, you know, we don't know
how long it can continue.

25:34.716 --> 25:38.246 align:middle
We have models, and we
look at this-we look

25:38.246 --> 25:39.846 align:middle
at this question all the time.

25:40.596 --> 25:42.706 align:middle
And, you know, admittedly,
we've been surprised

25:42.706 --> 25:45.536 align:middle
on the positive side here,
so we're open to the idea

25:45.536 --> 25:48.456 align:middle
that that may continue or not.

25:48.886 --> 25:50.706 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

25:51.926 --> 25:52.756 align:middle
Hi, Chair.

25:52.756 --> 25:54.546 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

25:54.546 --> 25:57.656 align:middle
Just to follow up on the tariff
question, you said earlier

25:57.656 --> 26:00.106 align:middle
that it seems as the
effects of the tariff

26:00.146 --> 26:02.676 align:middle
so far haven't quite
materialized-those weren't your

26:02.676 --> 26:06.896 align:middle
words verbat[im]-but Wal-Mart,
Gap, Coca-Cola, General Motors,

26:06.896 --> 26:09.476 align:middle
Macy's have all said that
they expect the tariffs

26:09.476 --> 26:12.256 align:middle
to affect the prices of
everyday consumer goods.

26:12.806 --> 26:16.796 align:middle
So my question is, when-should
there be an effect-when would

26:16.796 --> 26:18.036 align:middle
you expect to see that?

26:18.036 --> 26:20.386 align:middle
And at what point do you
start to become worried

26:20.636 --> 26:22.736 align:middle
that it's actually going
to affect consumer spending

26:22.736 --> 26:24.716 align:middle
and dampening economic growth?

26:25.406 --> 26:25.716 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

26:25.716 --> 26:26.336 align:middle
It's a concern.

26:26.336 --> 26:27.016 align:middle
It's a risk.

26:27.016 --> 26:32.486 align:middle
You know, so you could
see prices moving up.

26:32.736 --> 26:35.146 align:middle
You don't see it yet, but
we-you could see retail prices

26:35.146 --> 26:35.526 align:middle
moving up.

26:35.586 --> 26:38.346 align:middle
The tariffs might provide
a basis for companies

26:38.346 --> 26:42.126 align:middle
to raise prices in a world where
they've been very reluctant to

26:42.126 --> 26:43.446 align:middle
and unable to raise prices,

26:43.446 --> 26:45.786 align:middle
where raising prices is
really difficult, you know,

26:45.786 --> 26:47.286 align:middle
with the ability to
shop on the internet.

26:47.756 --> 26:50.976 align:middle
This could provide, you
know, a way for-again,

26:50.976 --> 26:52.136 align:middle
we don't see it in the numbers.

26:52.136 --> 26:54.486 align:middle
And, you know, until we
see it in the numbers,

26:54.486 --> 26:57.536 align:middle
it's hard to say
how one would react.

26:57.836 --> 27:00.226 align:middle
And the other question would
be, if you're just talking

27:00.226 --> 27:01.866 align:middle
about the effect
on inflation is,

27:02.306 --> 27:04.896 align:middle
is it just a one-time
increase in the price level,

27:05.046 --> 27:09.246 align:middle
or is it actually fueling
higher inflation going forward?

27:09.246 --> 27:11.536 align:middle
And that's a real-that's
an important question in,

27:11.576 --> 27:14.026 align:middle
in how we would think about
the appropriate response.

27:14.366 --> 27:14.656 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

27:14.656 --> 27:17.346 align:middle
But is there a sense of timing,
in terms of when you might see

27:17.346 --> 27:19.106 align:middle
that kick in, the effect of?

27:19.316 --> 27:19.686 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

27:19.946 --> 27:20.706 align:middle
Don't see it yet.

27:20.876 --> 27:23.406 align:middle
You know, don't see it yet,
but we-you're hearing-I mean,

27:23.406 --> 27:26.486 align:middle
broadly across the economy,
it's not just tariffs.

27:26.486 --> 27:30.576 align:middle
You're hearing-we're hearing a
lot of talk of labor shortages,

27:31.016 --> 27:33.026 align:middle
of material costs in
particular industries.

27:33.026 --> 27:33.956 align:middle
We're hearing a lot, you know,

27:34.356 --> 27:37.796 align:middle
what amount to supply-side
constraints and things

27:37.796 --> 27:40.016 align:middle
like that, but we're
not seeing it.

27:40.016 --> 27:42.866 align:middle
We're seeing a sort of
modest increase in wages,

27:43.436 --> 27:47.656 align:middle
inflation right around 2
percent, no sense of it moving

27:47.656 --> 27:50.276 align:middle
up really, so we're
not seeing it yet.

27:51.426 --> 27:53.576 align:middle
And, you know, we just aren't,

27:53.676 --> 27:55.166 align:middle
and we're watching
very carefully.

27:57.126 --> 27:58.406 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

27:59.106 --> 28:02.666 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Michael McKee,
Bloomberg Radio and Television.

28:03.036 --> 28:05.556 align:middle
Financial conditions are
still extremely loose

28:05.616 --> 28:08.166 align:middle
by all the measures
that track that.

28:08.476 --> 28:10.376 align:middle
The flow of funds
shows the decline

28:10.376 --> 28:12.826 align:middle
in short-term borrowing
other than in Treasuries.

28:12.826 --> 28:15.626 align:middle
So I'm wondering if that
suggests a change in the way

28:16.046 --> 28:18.136 align:middle
that tightening affects
the economy.

28:18.216 --> 28:21.156 align:middle
And along those lines,
you mentioned

28:21.156 --> 28:22.186 align:middle
that you dropped the word

28:22.186 --> 28:24.836 align:middle
"accommodative" doesn't
suggest a change in policy.

28:25.196 --> 28:28.206 align:middle
But if you're dropping
forward guidance in terms

28:28.256 --> 28:32.176 align:middle
of "accommodative," does the dot
plot really serve any purpose

28:32.176 --> 28:34.316 align:middle
anymore if, as you say,
you can't be confident

28:34.316 --> 28:37.306 align:middle
out to 2021 what's going
to happen with the economy?

28:38.446 --> 28:38.886 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

28:39.556 --> 28:41.276 align:middle
A couple questions in there.

28:42.206 --> 28:46.906 align:middle
So the dot plot-I
mean, I-it's not new.

28:46.906 --> 28:49.816 align:middle
I don't think it's a new
point that forecasts two

28:49.816 --> 28:52.856 align:middle
and three out-two or three
years out are fairly uncertain.

28:53.316 --> 28:57.176 align:middle
The dot plot is individual FOMC
participants who are writing

28:57.176 --> 29:02.096 align:middle
down their individual views on
the evolution of the economy

29:02.096 --> 29:03.486 align:middle
and of appropriate
monetary policy.

29:03.726 --> 29:04.776 align:middle
We don't vote on that.

29:04.906 --> 29:06.366 align:middle
These are individual forecasts.

29:06.366 --> 29:09.136 align:middle
I think market participants
generally find

29:09.136 --> 29:14.866 align:middle
that interesting information,
and so it seems to me

29:14.866 --> 29:15.786 align:middle
to be serving a purpose.

29:15.786 --> 29:19.086 align:middle
I think the point with
"accommodative" was

29:19.086 --> 29:21.866 align:middle
that its useful life was over.

29:21.866 --> 29:24.496 align:middle
You know, we put that in
the statement in 2015,

29:25.156 --> 29:27.856 align:middle
just when we lifted off, and the
idea was to provide assurance

29:27.856 --> 29:30.556 align:middle
that we weren't trying to slow
down the economy, but, in fact,

29:31.216 --> 29:34.876 align:middle
we were still-interest rates
were still going to be pushing

29:35.856 --> 29:37.696 align:middle
to support economic activity.

29:37.696 --> 29:39.916 align:middle
Well, that-you know, that
purpose has been well served,

29:39.916 --> 29:44.286 align:middle
and the language now doesn't
really say anything that's

29:44.286 --> 29:46.266 align:middle
important to the way the
Committee is thinking

29:46.266 --> 29:47.706 align:middle
about policy going forward.

29:47.706 --> 29:49.626 align:middle
That's why that came out.

29:50.006 --> 29:51.636 align:middle
Did I get-did I get
all of your ques-?

29:51.726 --> 29:52.256 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

29:52.366 --> 29:55.106 align:middle
The idea that financial
conditions remain loose even

29:55.106 --> 29:57.576 align:middle
though you raised
rates 200 basis points?

29:58.066 --> 29:58.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

29:58.446 --> 29:59.896 align:middle
Yeah, you know, it's-if you look

29:59.896 --> 30:01.556 align:middle
at a broad financial
conditions index,

30:01.556 --> 30:03.016 align:middle
that's pretty much
the answer you'll get.

30:03.046 --> 30:05.516 align:middle
We don't control that, and,
as I mentioned earlier,

30:05.516 --> 30:09.736 align:middle
we do take financial conditions
into account in what we do.

30:10.316 --> 30:12.706 align:middle
And financial conditions are
affecting the broader economy,

30:12.706 --> 30:15.036 align:middle
so, by definition, we're
taking them into account.

30:15.276 --> 30:16.936 align:middle
But if you look at-look at
interest rates, you know,

30:16.936 --> 30:19.356 align:middle
interest rates-look at long-term
interest rates this year

30:19.826 --> 30:21.006 align:middle
and short-term interest
rates this year,

30:21.006 --> 30:22.436 align:middle
there's very significant
increases.

30:22.996 --> 30:24.846 align:middle
And, you know, with your
long and variable lags,

30:24.846 --> 30:27.066 align:middle
that should be-that should be
having an effect over time.

30:27.436 --> 30:31.276 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

30:31.356 --> 30:33.026 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

30:34.226 --> 30:36.566 align:middle
So Nellie Liang was
just put forward

30:36.566 --> 30:39.076 align:middle
by President Donald
Trump to the Fed Board,

30:39.446 --> 30:41.336 align:middle
and I was just curious,
you know,

30:41.336 --> 30:43.696 align:middle
given that she's a
longtime veteran of the Fed,

30:43.696 --> 30:48.086 align:middle
whether you could speak to what
role you played in, you know,

30:48.336 --> 30:51.006 align:middle
recommending candidates to
the President, giving input

30:51.006 --> 30:52.286 align:middle
on who he should nominate.

30:52.786 --> 30:55.076 align:middle
And then also, if
I-if I may follow

30:55.076 --> 30:58.176 align:middle
up on the financial stability
discussion from earlier,

30:58.906 --> 31:02.556 align:middle
you know, you've mentioned
before that corporate debt is

31:02.556 --> 31:05.526 align:middle
at high levels, and I was
curious whether, you know,

31:05.526 --> 31:09.676 align:middle
you have any qualms about
loosening up restrictions

31:09.936 --> 31:15.316 align:middle
on leveraged lending by making
that guidance less of a-less

31:15.316 --> 31:18.236 align:middle
of a cudgel, I guess,
while-at a time

31:18.236 --> 31:19.816 align:middle
when leveraged lending
is gaining steam?

31:20.466 --> 31:21.176 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

31:21.206 --> 31:27.666 align:middle
Okay, so in terms of nominations
to the Fed, those are entirely,

31:27.786 --> 31:30.706 align:middle
completely under the control
of the White House and,

31:30.846 --> 31:31.886 align:middle
ultimately, the President.

31:32.146 --> 31:37.296 align:middle
And traditionally, the Fed Chair
has been consulted on those,

31:37.296 --> 31:39.056 align:middle
and I'm happy to say
that that has continued.

31:39.056 --> 31:41.256 align:middle
But these are-these are really
decisions for the White House,

31:41.256 --> 31:44.976 align:middle
and, you know, we are-think
of us as just being consulted.

31:45.256 --> 31:49.776 align:middle
I will say that I'm very happy
and excited about the team

31:49.776 --> 31:52.546 align:middle
that we're putting together, and
I'm very much looking forward

31:52.546 --> 31:56.726 align:middle
to having those offices up
and down our hall filled.

31:56.826 --> 31:59.616 align:middle
It's been pretty quiet,
more quieter than usual,

31:59.616 --> 32:01.726 align:middle
which is saying something
on the Board hall.

32:02.406 --> 32:05.536 align:middle
So we're looking forward to
getting some people confirmed.

32:06.326 --> 32:11.146 align:middle
In terms of corporate debt, so
this is a-this is a-you know,

32:11.206 --> 32:13.936 align:middle
I was an investor for
a long time in areas

32:13.936 --> 32:17.606 align:middle
that were very close to the
leveraged lending markets.

32:17.606 --> 32:19.746 align:middle
So, you know, I have had a
lot of experience in that.

32:19.796 --> 32:22.066 align:middle
That market has evolved
really significantly

32:22.066 --> 32:23.596 align:middle
since before the crisis.

32:24.356 --> 32:28.706 align:middle
And, you know, the banks
take much less risk

32:28.706 --> 32:29.416 align:middle
than they used to.

32:29.416 --> 32:32.756 align:middle
They're-the-they're
essentially in the business

32:32.756 --> 32:35.506 align:middle
of distributing these loans and
bonds rather than keeping them

32:35.506 --> 32:37.126 align:middle
on their balance sheet,
to a much greater degree.

32:37.536 --> 32:41.086 align:middle
But it's also true-and
there's significant research

32:41.086 --> 32:45.236 align:middle
on this-that excessive
risk-taking

32:45.236 --> 32:47.396 align:middle
in the leveraged lending
markets does have channels

32:47.396 --> 32:50.156 align:middle
for affecting the real
economy, if there's overheating

32:50.156 --> 32:51.266 align:middle
and that kind of thing
in those markets,

32:51.266 --> 32:52.426 align:middle
so we monitor that carefully.

32:53.146 --> 32:56.896 align:middle
You know, the guidance is-isn't
binding, as you pointed out,

32:56.896 --> 32:58.226 align:middle
that the leveraged
lending isn't binding,

32:58.226 --> 33:00.856 align:middle
and that's just-that's just the
way that the law is interpreted.

33:01.466 --> 33:05.646 align:middle
So it's something we're
monitoring, and, as I mentioned,

33:05.646 --> 33:08.056 align:middle
we think overall
vulnerabilities are moderate.

33:09.356 --> 33:11.136 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

33:14.836 --> 33:16.576 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

33:16.576 --> 33:19.856 align:middle
If I could come back to
"accommodative," do you think

33:19.856 --> 33:21.846 align:middle
of policy as accommodative
at present?

33:22.276 --> 33:24.896 align:middle
How close are you to neutrality,
and do you share the view

33:24.896 --> 33:25.816 align:middle
of some of your colleagues

33:25.816 --> 33:27.276 align:middle
that you'll eventually
need to be restrictive?

33:27.276 --> 33:28.586 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

33:28.676 --> 33:30.786 align:middle
Okay, so are we accommodative
now?

33:30.786 --> 33:34.526 align:middle
So I think if you look at
the-look at the dot plot

33:34.526 --> 33:38.186 align:middle
in the SEP, you'll see that
the federal funds rate,

33:38.446 --> 33:39.666 align:middle
even after today's move,

33:39.896 --> 33:42.606 align:middle
is below the longer-run
neutral estimate

33:42.606 --> 33:46.936 align:middle
of every single participant
that-who submits a-an estimate

33:46.936 --> 33:47.256 align:middle
of that.

33:47.256 --> 33:49.466 align:middle
So that's why this
is the perfect time

33:49.466 --> 33:51.446 align:middle
to take the language
out, because, you know,

33:51.446 --> 33:54.106 align:middle
it's perfectly clear that there
can't be a signal because,

33:54.696 --> 33:57.106 align:middle
you know, by definition, that
means an accommodative policy.

33:57.106 --> 33:59.676 align:middle
So it wasn't because
policy is not accommodative.

33:59.796 --> 34:01.106 align:middle
It is still accommodative.

34:01.696 --> 34:04.666 align:middle
The thinking was
more, as I mentioned,

34:04.666 --> 34:06.756 align:middle
it's more that the language
has run its useful life.

34:06.756 --> 34:10.506 align:middle
There's another point, though,
too, which is, you know,

34:10.506 --> 34:14.196 align:middle
we don't want to suggest either

34:14.196 --> 34:17.026 align:middle
that we have this precise
understanding of where

34:17.026 --> 34:19.926 align:middle
"accommodative" stops or suggest

34:19.926 --> 34:21.876 align:middle
that that's a really important
point in our thinking.

34:21.876 --> 34:23.916 align:middle
You know, what we're going
to be doing, assuming we stay

34:23.916 --> 34:25.406 align:middle
on this path, is, we're going

34:25.406 --> 34:27.976 align:middle
to be carefully monitoring
incoming data

34:27.976 --> 34:29.736 align:middle
from the financial markets
and from the economy

34:30.006 --> 34:32.526 align:middle
and asking ourselves whether our
policy is achieving the goals we

34:32.526 --> 34:34.446 align:middle
want to achieve-you know,
sustain the economy,

34:34.726 --> 34:36.346 align:middle
maximum employment,
stable prices.

34:36.346 --> 34:38.496 align:middle
That's the way we're thinking
about it, and that does kind

34:38.496 --> 34:40.686 align:middle
of amount to thinking less

34:40.686 --> 34:44.076 align:middle
about one's precise point
estimate of the neutral rate.

34:44.176 --> 34:45.556 align:middle
So that's how I think
about that.

34:46.156 --> 34:46.856 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

34:47.246 --> 34:48.996 align:middle
The second question was, do
you think you're going to end

34:48.996 --> 34:50.076 align:middle
up in a restrictive posture?

34:50.076 --> 34:52.116 align:middle
Governor Brainard, for example,
has said that she expects

34:52.176 --> 34:53.106 align:middle
that you will need to do that.

34:53.576 --> 34:54.846 align:middle
Do you agree with that?

34:54.926 --> 34:55.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

34:56.186 --> 34:56.996 align:middle
It's possible.

34:56.996 --> 34:57.796 align:middle
It's very possible.

34:57.796 --> 34:58.946 align:middle
It happens often.

34:59.076 --> 35:01.636 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, many of
the participants have written

35:01.636 --> 35:04.056 align:middle
that down, and they've written
down really modest overshoots,

35:04.056 --> 35:06.486 align:middle
you know, amounting to one
rate hike kind of thing,

35:06.546 --> 35:08.796 align:middle
which wouldn't have a big
effect on the economy.

35:09.396 --> 35:11.546 align:middle
You know, it-I would
just say, you know,

35:11.586 --> 35:14.046 align:middle
it really depends
on what happens.

35:14.146 --> 35:18.746 align:middle
It depends on if we
keep going-you can think

35:18.746 --> 35:19.596 align:middle
of it different ways.

35:19.816 --> 35:21.426 align:middle
Maybe we've underestimated
the neutral rate.

35:21.706 --> 35:23.596 align:middle
Maybe we'll be raising our
estimate of the neutral rate

35:23.596 --> 35:24.526 align:middle
and we'll just go to that,

35:24.866 --> 35:27.376 align:middle
or maybe we'll keep our neutral
rate here and then go one

35:27.376 --> 35:28.836 align:middle
or two rate increases beyond it.

35:29.596 --> 35:33.906 align:middle
I think it's very possible,
but I mean, ultimately,

35:34.716 --> 35:36.096 align:middle
isn't really the
question we're answering.

35:36.096 --> 35:37.276 align:middle
The question we're answering is,

35:37.426 --> 35:40.186 align:middle
how do we provide the
economy just the right amount

35:40.186 --> 35:43.986 align:middle
of support-not too much, not too
little-to sustain the recovery

35:44.686 --> 35:46.066 align:middle
and achieve our statutory goals?

35:46.956 --> 35:49.176 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

35:50.296 --> 35:51.196 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

35:51.256 --> 35:52.546 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

35:53.256 --> 35:56.176 align:middle
The 10-year anniversary
of the bankruptcy

35:56.176 --> 35:59.256 align:middle
of Lehman Brothers got a lot of
attention over the last month.

35:59.626 --> 36:02.016 align:middle
I was wondering if you
thought there were any lessons

36:02.016 --> 36:06.606 align:middle
from the crisis that you want
to share-and two points on that.

36:06.676 --> 36:09.516 align:middle
You go to Congress a lot and
talk to members of Congress.

36:09.516 --> 36:12.706 align:middle
Now, Secretary Paulson
and Mr. Geithner

36:12.706 --> 36:16.416 align:middle
and Chair Bernanke have said
that Congress made a mistake

36:16.866 --> 36:20.966 align:middle
when it took away the Fed's
emergency powers in Dodd-Frank.

36:21.146 --> 36:24.156 align:middle
And then one other thing
is that, are you confident

36:24.156 --> 36:27.246 align:middle
that all these nonbanks
didn't-you know,

36:27.246 --> 36:29.526 align:middle
nonbanks never got put
into the, you know,

36:29.526 --> 36:33.176 align:middle
the FSOC, you know, system.

36:33.176 --> 36:36.236 align:middle
So are you confident that
the-you can see the risks

36:36.236 --> 36:37.136 align:middle
in the financial system?

36:37.526 --> 36:37.776 align:middle
Thank you.

36:38.336 --> 36:38.916 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

36:39.816 --> 36:40.776 align:middle
So, three things there.

36:40.856 --> 36:45.886 align:middle
The first thing is the
single biggest thing I think

36:45.886 --> 36:48.726 align:middle
that we learned was the-as I
mentioned, I think, earlier,

36:49.226 --> 36:52.616 align:middle
the importance of
maintaining the stability

36:52.616 --> 36:53.716 align:middle
of the financial system.

36:54.086 --> 36:55.856 align:middle
So I think if you
look-if you look back

36:55.856 --> 36:57.956 align:middle
at the way the models worked
and the way people thought

36:57.956 --> 37:00.586 align:middle
about risk in the economy,
that's what was missing,

37:00.586 --> 37:02.436 align:middle
and I think it's
not missing anymore.

37:02.436 --> 37:05.266 align:middle
So we-you know, we put a
tremendous focus on that.

37:05.266 --> 37:07.046 align:middle
We raised capital, liquidity.

37:07.366 --> 37:09.326 align:middle
We have stress tests,
which force banks

37:09.326 --> 37:12.006 align:middle
to understand-the largest banks
particularly-to understand

37:12.006 --> 37:14.326 align:middle
and better manage their
risk and have enough capital

37:14.326 --> 37:18.286 align:middle
to survive a really substantial
shock that's at least as bad

37:18.286 --> 37:19.376 align:middle
as the financial crisis.

37:19.796 --> 37:22.476 align:middle
And if all that doesn't work,
we've got resolution plans,

37:22.476 --> 37:24.686 align:middle
which we've, you know,
through many cycles,

37:24.686 --> 37:26.706 align:middle
have made really
substantial progress,

37:26.706 --> 37:28.906 align:middle
more than I had thought
was likely.

37:28.906 --> 37:30.086 align:middle
So we've done a lot.

37:30.286 --> 37:34.156 align:middle
Nobody's-you know, nobody
is, I think, overconfident

37:34.156 --> 37:35.516 align:middle
that we've solved every problem.

37:35.976 --> 37:37.706 align:middle
And, you know, now we're
going back and kind

37:37.706 --> 37:41.086 align:middle
of taking a hard look at
everything and, you know,

37:41.086 --> 37:43.616 align:middle
trying to make-trying
to-trying to keep at it.

37:43.616 --> 37:46.606 align:middle
So I think those are the
really important lessons, and,

37:46.606 --> 37:48.756 align:middle
you know, we-we're
determined not to,

37:49.836 --> 37:50.776 align:middle
you know, not to forget them.

37:51.066 --> 37:55.426 align:middle
And that's, I think, a risk
now-is to-is to forget things

37:55.426 --> 37:56.266 align:middle
that we-that we learned.

37:56.266 --> 37:57.506 align:middle
That's just human
nature over time.

37:58.086 --> 38:00.736 align:middle
I saw the article you're
talking about; the question is,

38:00.876 --> 38:04.276 align:middle
did Congress take things away,
emergency powers from the Fed?

38:04.276 --> 38:08.636 align:middle
So there was sort of a trade for
taking away our power under 13,

38:08.636 --> 38:13.936 align:middle
section 13(3), to provide
support to individual nonbanks,

38:13.936 --> 38:16.376 align:middle
which is really holding
companies and other companies.

38:16.376 --> 38:21.846 align:middle
In exchange for that, we got
orderly liquidation authority

38:21.846 --> 38:23.046 align:middle
and a resolution authority.

38:23.556 --> 38:24.406 align:middle
Was it a mistake?

38:24.606 --> 38:25.966 align:middle
I don't think there's any sense

38:25.966 --> 38:28.886 align:middle
that Congress would seriously
look at changing that.

38:29.666 --> 38:33.536 align:middle
I have real doubts about
whether it was wise

38:33.536 --> 38:36.926 align:middle
to take away our
crisis-fighting tools.

38:37.276 --> 38:39.696 align:middle
I think you put them away, and
you hope you never need them,

38:39.696 --> 38:43.746 align:middle
but-and I certainly
strongly oppose efforts

38:43.746 --> 38:45.816 align:middle
to take away more of our tools.

38:46.246 --> 38:49.656 align:middle
You know, the third thing
you mentioned is the

38:49.656 --> 38:50.616 align:middle
designation power.

38:50.616 --> 38:52.136 align:middle
So it's a really
important power.

38:52.536 --> 38:54.706 align:middle
In my mind, it should
be used-my thinking,

38:54.706 --> 38:58.416 align:middle
it should be used sparingly,
and that means, you know,

38:58.416 --> 39:00.886 align:middle
in situations where you
have-I mean, in principle,

39:02.676 --> 39:05.626 align:middle
you could have another
Lehman Brothers come up out

39:05.626 --> 39:07.476 align:middle
of the ground that's not a bank,

39:08.066 --> 39:09.946 align:middle
and it could be very
threatening-or it could be

39:09.996 --> 39:11.506 align:middle
capable of creating
systemic risk.

39:11.506 --> 39:13.406 align:middle
And so I think it's a
critical power to have.

39:13.446 --> 39:14.736 align:middle
But, again, I would
use it-I would tend

39:14.736 --> 39:17.026 align:middle
to use it fairly sparingly.

39:17.146 --> 39:20.016 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:21.616 --> 39:23.496 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

39:23.856 --> 39:25.346 align:middle
Are you worried about the effect

39:25.346 --> 39:28.126 align:middle
of rising interest
rates on consumers?

39:28.176 --> 39:31.696 align:middle
Credit card rates have jumped
to more than 17 percent

39:32.086 --> 39:34.396 align:middle
since the Fed started
raising interest rates.

39:34.396 --> 39:35.526 align:middle
Mortgage rates are up.

39:35.936 --> 39:37.096 align:middle
What do you say to consumers?

39:38.016 --> 39:38.566 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

39:38.976 --> 39:41.806 align:middle
Yeah, we look-of course, we
look at that very carefully,

39:41.986 --> 39:45.276 align:middle
and interest rates are going
up across a broad range

39:46.356 --> 39:48.916 align:middle
of consumer borrowing,
as they would

39:48.916 --> 39:51.336 align:middle
when we raise short-term
interest rates.

39:51.816 --> 39:54.296 align:middle
They're still quite low
by historical levels.

39:54.906 --> 39:58.356 align:middle
And the other thing I'll say
is, take-if you take housing,

39:58.546 --> 40:02.206 align:middle
if you look at the NAR
Affordability Index,

40:02.706 --> 40:05.656 align:middle
housing is still
more affordable now

40:05.656 --> 40:07.516 align:middle
than it was before
the financial crisis.

40:08.046 --> 40:11.606 align:middle
So you're right that the cost
of borrowing is going up,

40:11.926 --> 40:14.956 align:middle
but it's going up from what
were extraordinarily low levels,

40:15.106 --> 40:17.436 align:middle
and it is something
that we watch carefully.

40:17.496 --> 40:20.066 align:middle
You know, the effect on consumer
spending is a very important

40:20.606 --> 40:22.066 align:middle
channel through which
interest rates work,

40:22.066 --> 40:23.596 align:middle
and so we do monitor
the effects.

40:24.676 --> 40:26.176 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

40:27.036 --> 40:28.506 align:middle
Hi, Hannah Lang,
American Banker.

40:28.556 --> 40:31.886 align:middle
OCC's decision to go
at CRA alone suggests

40:31.886 --> 40:34.316 align:middle
that there's some sort
of disagreement among how

40:34.316 --> 40:35.706 align:middle
to proceed with a joint plan.

40:35.706 --> 40:39.136 align:middle
What are the issues that
are preventing a joint plan

40:39.136 --> 40:41.186 align:middle
from developing, and
how close are you

40:41.386 --> 40:42.756 align:middle
to a possible joint plan?

40:43.226 --> 40:44.126 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

40:44.206 --> 40:47.376 align:middle
Well, at the Fed, we are,
you know, deeply committed

40:47.376 --> 40:51.876 align:middle
to the mission of CRA, which
is for banks to provide credit

40:51.876 --> 40:55.226 align:middle
and other banking services in
the communities they serve.

40:56.086 --> 40:59.266 align:middle
And we have also been of
the view for some time

40:59.266 --> 41:01.676 align:middle
that with the evolution
of technology particularly

41:02.096 --> 41:04.016 align:middle
and changes-changes in
the nature of banking,

41:04.016 --> 41:07.626 align:middle
that it's an appropriate
time-it's past an appropriate

41:07.626 --> 41:09.166 align:middle
time, but now it's
certainly an appropriate time

41:09.166 --> 41:11.776 align:middle
to revisit the way
we think about CRA.

41:12.316 --> 41:17.456 align:middle
But we don't want to lose
that focus on community, and,

41:17.456 --> 41:19.796 align:middle
you know, we definitely
want to see

41:19.796 --> 41:23.146 align:middle
that fundamental purpose
of the law sustained.

41:24.026 --> 41:26.446 align:middle
Many of the issues that
we had were reflected

41:26.446 --> 41:32.806 align:middle
in the OCC's ANPR, and
we're-we're hopeful that,

41:32.876 --> 41:36.586 align:middle
over time, there will be a
joint proposed rulemaking

41:36.686 --> 41:38.856 align:middle
with the OCC, the
FDIC, and the Fed.

41:39.596 --> 41:42.146 align:middle
It's a process, and, you know,
we're very much interested

41:42.146 --> 41:43.766 align:middle
in continuing to
push it forward.

41:44.256 --> 41:46.666 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

41:47.746 --> 41:50.886 align:middle
Steve Beckner, freelance
journalist filing for NPR.

41:50.886 --> 41:53.616 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, following

41:53.616 --> 41:57.716 align:middle
up on the neutral rate issue-and
you touched on this-but,

41:57.716 --> 42:01.776 align:middle
you know, you and your
colleagues slightly lifted the

42:01.776 --> 42:05.576 align:middle
longer-run estimate, the
neutral funds rate estimate

42:05.976 --> 42:08.776 align:middle
to 3 percent, but
that's still, I believe,

42:08.776 --> 42:14.276 align:middle
like 125 basis points below
where it was six years ago.

42:14.776 --> 42:18.756 align:middle
What are the odds that
productivity, you know,

42:18.756 --> 42:20.846 align:middle
investment, productivity,
labor market,

42:20.846 --> 42:27.836 align:middle
other developments could further
push up the longer-run estimate

42:28.316 --> 42:33.886 align:middle
and possibly make the
projected funds rate levels less

42:33.886 --> 42:35.406 align:middle
restrictive than they now seem?

42:36.406 --> 42:37.156 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

42:37.156 --> 42:41.696 align:middle
So, in principle, the-these
starred variables-the long-run

42:41.696 --> 42:43.986 align:middle
growth rate of the economy,
the neutral rate of interest,

42:43.986 --> 42:46.396 align:middle
the natural rate of
unemployment-don't move quickly.

42:46.396 --> 42:47.796 align:middle
They move very gradually.

42:47.796 --> 42:49.906 align:middle
They're pinned down
by longer-run forces,

42:49.906 --> 42:50.976 align:middle
like demographics.

42:50.976 --> 42:52.256 align:middle
In the case of the funds rate,

42:52.426 --> 42:55.496 align:middle
it's more-it's productivity-it's
appetite for safe assets

42:56.456 --> 42:58.366 align:middle
and demographics and
other things, too.

42:58.816 --> 43:02.546 align:middle
So they move quite
slowly through time.

43:02.546 --> 43:04.106 align:middle
I think we all look
at them-those of us

43:04.106 --> 43:07.176 align:middle
who file SEPs every quarter
will look at that every quarter

43:07.176 --> 43:08.856 align:middle
and not make a change every
quarter but, every now

43:08.856 --> 43:09.826 align:middle
and again, make a change.

43:09.866 --> 43:14.626 align:middle
And you're right-we actually-the
median ticked up a tenth.

43:15.246 --> 43:17.396 align:middle
It's a tenth we're talking
about here, so-but still,

43:17.396 --> 43:18.736 align:middle
I think it's a positive thing

43:19.046 --> 43:21.046 align:middle
that people could be
raising their estimates

43:21.106 --> 43:23.496 align:middle
of the longer-run neutral rate

43:23.646 --> 43:25.896 align:middle
and potentially the
longer-run growth rate.

43:25.896 --> 43:26.276 align:middle
Who knows?

43:26.276 --> 43:29.976 align:middle
And maybe reducing their
level of the natural rate

43:29.976 --> 43:31.366 align:middle
of unemployment, which
has been the trend.

43:31.936 --> 43:34.766 align:middle
It's just that you change
those estimates over time.

43:34.766 --> 43:36.336 align:middle
They're informed
by incoming data.

43:36.806 --> 43:37.956 align:middle
They don't change a lot, though,

43:38.126 --> 43:39.686 align:middle
although they did-it's
interesting

43:39.686 --> 43:41.806 align:middle
that the neutral rate
changed quite sharply

43:41.806 --> 43:45.106 align:middle
after the financial crisis
in many different models,

43:45.506 --> 43:46.916 align:middle
and it's been slow to recover.

43:47.716 --> 43:51.466 align:middle
I don't expect a dramatic
recovery there, but, you know,

43:51.466 --> 43:54.286 align:middle
we-we're so bad at
forecasting productivity.

43:54.286 --> 43:56.416 align:middle
It's just very hard to know
when productivity is going

43:56.416 --> 43:57.706 align:middle
to arrive and in what quantity.

43:58.436 --> 44:02.636 align:middle
So I think we have to be humble
about how little we really know

44:02.756 --> 44:05.426 align:middle
about where these starred
value variables either are

44:05.516 --> 44:07.396 align:middle
or are going.

44:08.086 --> 44:08.976 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

44:11.116 --> 44:11.976 align:middle
Hi, Chairman.

44:11.976 --> 44:13.906 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

44:13.906 --> 44:15.236 align:middle
Thank you for taking
the question.

44:15.726 --> 44:19.866 align:middle
I'd like to just kind of ask
again-you mentioned earlier

44:19.866 --> 44:21.966 align:middle
that gradual-a gradual pace

44:21.966 --> 44:24.696 align:middle
of rate increases will
make it easier to react.

44:25.406 --> 44:28.386 align:middle
But, specifically, what are
you going to be looking to see,

44:28.386 --> 44:30.126 align:middle
because you've also
mentioned, you know,

44:30.126 --> 44:31.986 align:middle
that monetary policy
operates with a lag,

44:32.636 --> 44:35.336 align:middle
so any kind of specific
signposts

44:35.336 --> 44:38.416 align:middle
that maybe we've reached the
end of the tightening cycle

44:38.416 --> 44:40.556 align:middle
or where we should stop?

44:40.976 --> 44:44.266 align:middle
Secondly, if you worry
about the divergence

44:44.266 --> 44:47.816 align:middle
between interest rates in
the U.S. and internationally,

44:47.816 --> 44:48.606 align:middle
especially at a time

44:48.606 --> 44:51.936 align:middle
when emerging markets
are under pressure.

44:52.106 --> 44:54.336 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

44:54.336 --> 44:56.926 align:middle
Sure. So, you know, some of
the things that we'd be looking

44:56.926 --> 45:01.426 align:middle
at to tell us whether
we're getting close

45:01.426 --> 45:04.656 align:middle
to supply-side limits
would be, first,

45:04.876 --> 45:07.186 align:middle
does job growth slow down?

45:07.336 --> 45:10.906 align:middle
A slowing down in job growth
would be an indicator, you know.

45:10.906 --> 45:14.476 align:middle
An unexpectedly sharp
increase in wages

45:14.936 --> 45:19.276 align:middle
or inflation could tell you that
you're reaching those points.

45:19.436 --> 45:22.246 align:middle
You know, if headline growth
slowed down, that's another one.

45:22.246 --> 45:25.686 align:middle
So all of those things would be
worth taking into consideration.

45:25.766 --> 45:32.466 align:middle
I think also, though, we've
seen sometimes sharp tightening

45:32.466 --> 45:37.486 align:middle
in financial conditions, as we
saw at the beginning of 2016,

45:38.076 --> 45:42.166 align:middle
can have a substantial effect
pretty quickly on our economy

45:42.256 --> 45:44.396 align:middle
if it's a broad and
significant tightening.

45:44.396 --> 45:46.366 align:middle
So we would be looking
for those kinds of things

45:47.236 --> 45:48.456 align:middle
and many other things.

45:49.556 --> 45:52.046 align:middle
So, in terms of divergence, yes.

45:52.046 --> 45:56.586 align:middle
So the U.S. economy is, as I
mentioned, is strong and is

45:56.586 --> 46:00.586 align:middle
at a pretty positive moment,
you know, with strong growth,

46:00.586 --> 46:05.156 align:middle
healthy labor market, inflation
right on target, as I mentioned.

46:05.156 --> 46:09.276 align:middle
So-and we've seen growth
abroad, but growth, I think,

46:09.346 --> 46:11.016 align:middle
both in advanced economies

46:11.016 --> 46:14.736 align:middle
and in some emerging economies
has slowed down a little bit.

46:14.736 --> 46:19.926 align:middle
And in that world, you know,
if the U.S. is stronger,

46:20.166 --> 46:23.406 align:middle
then that's just part of the
context, part of the environment

46:23.406 --> 46:24.806 align:middle
that could mean a higher dollar.

46:24.806 --> 46:26.896 align:middle
It could be-it could
therefore mean that some

46:26.896 --> 46:32.426 align:middle
of our demand will wind up being
shared with other economies.

46:32.426 --> 46:35.676 align:middle
That's the way the integrated
international economy works.

46:36.416 --> 46:39.736 align:middle
So you could see that, and, you
know, it's just-it's just all

46:39.736 --> 46:44.226 align:middle
of the-all things that go
into thinking about, you know,

46:44.396 --> 46:45.516 align:middle
the path of the economy.

46:45.786 --> 46:48.576 align:middle
I would say, you know, we're
not responsible for the dollar.

46:48.736 --> 46:50.646 align:middle
Of course, the Treasury
is responsible

46:50.646 --> 46:54.506 align:middle
for managing the dollar, but the
dollar has only partly recovered

46:54.646 --> 46:56.776 align:middle
the decline that it had in 2017.

46:56.776 --> 47:00.016 align:middle
It's moved up off its lows,
but it's not as high as it was

47:00.016 --> 47:02.656 align:middle
at the beginning of last year
yet by a significant margin.

47:04.336 --> 47:05.736 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

47:10.596 --> 47:13.086 align:middle
Hi. Jean Yung with
Market News International.

47:13.796 --> 47:17.146 align:middle
Can you give us an update
on the technical adjustment

47:17.146 --> 47:18.996 align:middle
in implementing monetary policy

47:18.996 --> 47:20.686 align:middle
that the Fed made
earlier this year

47:21.396 --> 47:24.536 align:middle
when it decoupled the interest
on excess reserves rate

47:24.536 --> 47:26.096 align:middle
from the upper bound
of the target range?

47:26.096 --> 47:28.316 align:middle
And have you been happy
with that adjustment?

47:28.316 --> 47:30.616 align:middle
And do you see the
need to make another

47:30.616 --> 47:32.016 align:middle
such adjustment in
the near future?

47:32.016 --> 47:33.056 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

47:33.456 --> 47:37.306 align:middle
So we've said that we would
use our tools to assure

47:37.306 --> 47:39.326 align:middle
that the federal funds
rate trains-trades

47:39.326 --> 47:40.356 align:middle
within the target range.

47:41.046 --> 47:43.836 align:middle
And the principal tools
we use have been interest

47:43.836 --> 47:46.006 align:middle
on excess reserves and
then, at the bottom end,

47:46.006 --> 47:48.016 align:middle
a reverse repo facility.

47:48.476 --> 47:51.336 align:middle
So we-in June, when
we raised rates,

47:51.336 --> 47:53.936 align:middle
we only raised the
interest on excess reserves

47:53.966 --> 47:56.476 align:middle
by 20 basis points and
raised the range by 25,

47:56.476 --> 47:59.916 align:middle
and that moved federal funds
sort of back into the range.

48:00.376 --> 48:02.906 align:middle
It worked, and it
was successful.

48:02.906 --> 48:06.006 align:middle
The federal funds rate
traded in the low 190s,

48:06.076 --> 48:07.216 align:middle
well within the range.

48:07.876 --> 48:10.226 align:middle
We may-we may do that again.

48:10.226 --> 48:13.676 align:middle
You know, we-again, we have our
tools, and we will use them.

48:13.676 --> 48:17.226 align:middle
We think it's principally
a function just of a number

48:18.046 --> 48:20.336 align:middle
of things, but particularly
high Treasury supply,

48:20.336 --> 48:23.676 align:middle
and-which is showing up in
repo rates and then showing

48:23.676 --> 48:24.796 align:middle
up in fed funds as well.

48:24.796 --> 48:27.866 align:middle
So we don't see it as
a-as a big problem.

48:27.866 --> 48:29.896 align:middle
We see it as a problem we
can address with our tools,

48:29.896 --> 48:35.016 align:middle
and we'll use them
if we have to.

48:37.836 --> 48:39.236 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

48:40.356 --> 48:40.906 align:middle
Hi, Chairman.

48:41.056 --> 48:42.626 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

48:42.626 --> 48:46.496 align:middle
Last month, the CEA
outlined a different way

48:46.496 --> 48:47.566 align:middle
to calculate wage growth.

48:48.026 --> 48:51.046 align:middle
Has the Fed looked at that at
all, and what are you seeing

48:51.046 --> 48:53.906 align:middle
in terms of nonwage-based
compensation?

48:54.226 --> 48:54.836 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

48:54.886 --> 48:56.566 align:middle
So I wouldn't comment
on a CEA paper,

48:56.566 --> 48:58.266 align:middle
and I would imagine they
probably wouldn't comment

48:58.266 --> 49:00.686 align:middle
on our stuff, either, but
I would just say this.

49:00.686 --> 49:03.016 align:middle
So I am familiar with the
question and the work.

49:03.436 --> 49:06.946 align:middle
You know, we look-we look at a
range of indicators of wages,

49:07.436 --> 49:10.386 align:middle
and I think the broader,
the better.

49:10.386 --> 49:13.356 align:middle
You really-in a perfect-a
perfect wage measure would also

49:13.356 --> 49:16.806 align:middle
include benefits, because a lot
of compensation these days shows

49:16.806 --> 49:18.586 align:middle
up in the form of
benefits rather than wages,

49:18.586 --> 49:21.756 align:middle
so I think it's right to
choose those broader ones.

49:21.756 --> 49:23.936 align:middle
But they're kind of all telling
the same story right now.

49:24.286 --> 49:27.726 align:middle
If you look at the principal
four that we look at-we look

49:27.726 --> 49:29.356 align:middle
at a whole range of wage

49:29.356 --> 49:33.486 align:middle
and benefit cost indicators-they
are all now showing wages

49:33.486 --> 49:35.716 align:middle
and benefits around 3
percent growth, right?

49:35.876 --> 49:36.986 align:middle
Clustered around 3 percent.

49:37.226 --> 49:38.506 align:middle
That's a full percentage
point higher

49:38.506 --> 49:39.586 align:middle
than it was five years ago.

49:39.796 --> 49:40.346 align:middle
That's a good thing.

49:40.746 --> 49:42.216 align:middle
Then the question-the
other question is,

49:42.776 --> 49:43.716 align:middle
what about inflation?

49:43.716 --> 49:46.076 align:middle
You know, if you're-if you're
looking for real wage increases,

49:46.076 --> 49:47.956 align:middle
you've got to ask
inflation, and there,

49:47.956 --> 49:49.446 align:middle
you have to pick an
inflation measure.

49:49.746 --> 49:51.136 align:middle
Some people pick CPI.

49:51.236 --> 49:54.106 align:middle
We, of course, pick personal
consumption expenditures

49:54.156 --> 49:56.476 align:middle
because we think it's a
little better measure.

49:56.476 --> 49:59.426 align:middle
It's a little broader, and it
tends to run a little bit lower

49:59.426 --> 50:00.636 align:middle
as well, but that's
not why we pick it.

50:00.636 --> 50:01.966 align:middle
We just think it's
more accurate.

50:02.376 --> 50:04.996 align:middle
And that-the trend there is
running around 2 percent,

50:05.356 --> 50:08.316 align:middle
so if wages are running at
rough-wages and benefits running

50:08.316 --> 50:09.756 align:middle
at roughly 3 percent

50:10.506 --> 50:13.346 align:middle
and inflation running
at around 2 percent.

50:14.536 --> 50:17.106 align:middle
PCE headline inflation
is at 2.3 percent.

50:17.346 --> 50:22.066 align:middle
PCE core is at about 2, and I
think we see that, you know,

50:22.196 --> 50:26.086 align:middle
the temporary increase
in headline inflation

50:26.086 --> 50:27.896 align:middle
as being a function of
oil prices probably,

50:27.896 --> 50:29.916 align:middle
and we expect inflation to
go back down to 2 percent.

50:29.916 --> 50:31.196 align:middle
So we think of that
as 2 percent.

50:31.576 --> 50:32.536 align:middle
That's how we think about it.

50:32.606 --> 50:34.556 align:middle
Is that-I hope that helps.

50:34.836 --> 50:36.286 align:middle
MYLES UDLAND.

50:36.676 --> 50:40.776 align:middle
Myles Udland, Yahoo Finance.

50:40.776 --> 50:43.786 align:middle
Chair Powell, I want to follow
up on something you said,

50:43.786 --> 50:44.896 align:middle
actually, at Jackson Hole.

50:45.056 --> 50:45.706 align:middle
You were talking

50:45.706 --> 50:48.156 align:middle
about longer-term structural
challenges for the economy,

50:48.356 --> 50:49.036 align:middle
and you mentioned

50:49.036 --> 50:50.876 align:middle
that addressing the
federal budget deficit,

50:50.876 --> 50:52.736 align:middle
which has long been on
an unsustainable path,

50:52.736 --> 50:55.046 align:middle
becomes increasingly
important as a larger share

50:55.046 --> 50:56.286 align:middle
of the population retires.

50:56.626 --> 50:58.816 align:middle
I'm just wondering if you
could, you know, maybe expand

50:58.816 --> 51:01.126 align:middle
on your thoughts there and if
this is an issue that comes

51:01.126 --> 51:04.366 align:middle
up among members
of the FOMC at all.

51:04.366 --> 51:06.056 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

51:06.866 --> 51:08.406 align:middle
So it doesn't really come up.

51:08.406 --> 51:10.276 align:middle
It's not really our job.

51:10.916 --> 51:15.406 align:middle
And we do-we do monetary policy.

51:15.406 --> 51:17.296 align:middle
We regulate financial
institutions,

51:17.296 --> 51:19.276 align:middle
financial stability,
that sort of thing.

51:19.276 --> 51:21.036 align:middle
We don't have responsibility
for fiscal policy.

51:21.116 --> 51:22.326 align:middle
But in the longer-run,

51:22.326 --> 51:24.176 align:middle
fiscal policy will have
a significant effect

51:24.176 --> 51:26.716 align:middle
on the economy, so
for that reason,

51:26.716 --> 51:30.506 align:middle
I think my predecessors have
commented on fiscal policy.

51:30.506 --> 51:33.396 align:middle
But they've commented on-at a
high level rather than trying

51:33.396 --> 51:37.396 align:middle
to get involved in particular,
you know, particular measures.

51:37.396 --> 51:39.886 align:middle
And so-and I-my plan
is to kind of stick

51:39.886 --> 51:42.306 align:middle
to the same approach
and stay in our lane.

51:42.616 --> 51:45.776 align:middle
And, you know-so I would just
say, it's-it's no secret,

51:45.776 --> 51:47.186 align:middle
it's been true for a long time

51:47.186 --> 51:51.106 align:middle
that with our uniquely expensive
health-care delivery system

51:51.106 --> 51:52.536 align:middle
and the aging of our population,

51:52.886 --> 51:55.396 align:middle
we've been on an unsustainable
fiscal path for a long time,

51:56.106 --> 51:58.906 align:middle
and there's no hiding from it.

51:58.906 --> 52:02.716 align:middle
In the end, we will have to face
that, and I think, you know,

52:03.086 --> 52:05.286 align:middle
the sooner, the better.

52:05.286 --> 52:06.976 align:middle
And also, these are good times.

52:06.976 --> 52:09.976 align:middle
These are, you know, this
is-this is the economy

52:09.976 --> 52:11.436 align:middle
at nearly full employment

52:11.436 --> 52:13.386 align:middle
or in the range-in the
neighborhood of full employment.

52:14.276 --> 52:15.206 align:middle
Interest rates are low.

52:15.206 --> 52:17.656 align:middle
It's a-it's a good time to
be addressing these things.

52:17.656 --> 52:21.826 align:middle
So I just-I put that out
there and leave it at that.

52:21.966 --> 52:23.406 align:middle
TAKESHI KAWANAMI.

52:23.726 --> 52:26.526 align:middle
I'm Takeshi Kawanami
from Nikkei newspaper.

52:26.526 --> 52:28.266 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

52:28.266 --> 52:31.026 align:middle
I'd like to ask about
emerging markets.

52:31.216 --> 52:34.726 align:middle
What do you think about
impact of U.S. monetary policy

52:34.856 --> 52:38.146 align:middle
on emerging markets-interest
rate, currency depreciation,

52:38.146 --> 52:40.456 align:middle
or capital outflow from
emerging countries?

52:41.276 --> 52:43.326 align:middle
And do you think
there is a possibility

52:43.326 --> 52:46.256 align:middle
to stop the interest
rate hike due

52:46.256 --> 52:50.996 align:middle
to emerging markets turmoil-due
to turmoil in emerging markets?

52:51.356 --> 52:52.126 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

52:52.126 --> 52:53.196 align:middle
Sorry, the second question was?

52:53.196 --> 52:53.946 align:middle
TAKESHI KAWANAMI.

52:53.946 --> 52:56.046 align:middle
There is a possibility
to stop the .

52:56.046 --> 52:56.616 align:middle
. . CHAIRMAN POWELL.

52:56.616 --> 52:57.366 align:middle
Oh, to stop.

52:57.746 --> 52:58.536 align:middle
TAKESHI KAWANAMI.

52:58.806 --> 52:58.906 align:middle
. . . hike.

52:58.986 --> 52:59.766 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

52:59.766 --> 53:02.936 align:middle
Well, we serve a domestic
mandate, which is assigned to us

53:02.936 --> 53:06.846 align:middle
by Congress, and that is
to have maximum employment

53:06.846 --> 53:07.766 align:middle
and stable prices.

53:09.056 --> 53:12.696 align:middle
About half of global GDP is
outside the United States,

53:12.696 --> 53:13.606 align:middle
and way more than half

53:13.606 --> 53:15.986 align:middle
of the growth is
outside the United States

53:15.986 --> 53:18.286 align:middle
and in emerging markets,
I should say.

53:18.706 --> 53:19.966 align:middle
And so the performance

53:19.966 --> 53:24.436 align:middle
in the emerging market economies
really, really matters to us

53:24.906 --> 53:26.416 align:middle
in carrying out our
domestic mandate.

53:27.096 --> 53:30.066 align:middle
I'd also say that, you
know, a strong U.S. economy,

53:30.066 --> 53:31.716 align:middle
where Americans are
buying things

53:31.716 --> 53:34.766 align:middle
and the economy is growing
and our-you know, that's going

53:34.766 --> 53:38.026 align:middle
to support demand all around the
globe, so that's a good thing.

53:38.826 --> 53:40.836 align:middle
Now, we do understand, though,

53:40.836 --> 53:44.216 align:middle
that when we-when our economy is
strong and we're raising rates,

53:44.736 --> 53:47.346 align:middle
that puts upward pressure on
interest rates around the world

53:47.346 --> 53:49.216 align:middle
and can affect countries,
particularly countries

53:49.216 --> 53:51.856 align:middle
that have significant
external dollar borrowing.

53:52.366 --> 53:55.696 align:middle
And what we try to do
is be very transparent

53:55.696 --> 53:57.966 align:middle
about what we're doing and why.

53:58.646 --> 53:59.956 align:middle
And we have been, I believe,

54:00.146 --> 54:01.696 align:middle
and we've also moved
quite gradually.

54:01.696 --> 54:07.776 align:middle
So I-you know, I think
we've been performing well

54:07.776 --> 54:08.326 align:middle
on that front.

54:09.206 --> 54:10.146 align:middle
There are some countries

54:10.146 --> 54:11.896 align:middle
that are-that are
undergoing severe stress,

54:11.896 --> 54:15.616 align:middle
a handful of them, and-but not
most emerging market countries.

54:15.616 --> 54:17.036 align:middle
It's a relatively small number,

54:17.036 --> 54:19.626 align:middle
and those particular
countries have, you know,

54:19.626 --> 54:22.196 align:middle
have particular vulnerabilities,
which are well known in the form

54:22.196 --> 54:23.246 align:middle
of budget deficits,

54:23.346 --> 54:26.326 align:middle
and significant external
dollar borrowings,

54:26.326 --> 54:28.806 align:middle
and high inflation,
and things like that.

54:29.066 --> 54:32.616 align:middle
So, again, we'll continue to
conduct U.S. monetary policy

54:32.616 --> 54:36.096 align:middle
as transparently
as we possibly can,

54:36.166 --> 54:37.686 align:middle
and that's really the
best thing we can do,

54:37.686 --> 54:39.146 align:middle
along with supporting
U.S. growth.

54:40.226 --> 54:43.776 align:middle
I'll just-I think that answers
both your questions, actually.

54:44.766 --> 54:46.556 align:middle
VIRGINIE MONTET.

54:46.636 --> 54:50.206 align:middle
Virginie Montet with
Agence France-Presse.

54:50.206 --> 54:53.926 align:middle
You mentioned earlier a
possible, not probable,

54:53.926 --> 54:56.206 align:middle
correction, and I
want to come back

54:56.206 --> 54:59.396 align:middle
to the high level
of the stock market.

54:59.396 --> 55:03.456 align:middle
Are there any participants
to the FOMC to think

55:03.456 --> 55:07.456 align:middle
that we are witnessing an
episode of irrational exuberance

55:07.736 --> 55:09.156 align:middle
or rational exuberance?

55:10.036 --> 55:13.306 align:middle
And if there was a
steep correction,

55:13.306 --> 55:16.156 align:middle
would it provoke financial
stability concerns?

55:16.686 --> 55:17.996 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

55:18.306 --> 55:20.846 align:middle
So I don't comment on
the appropriateness

55:20.846 --> 55:22.726 align:middle
of the level of stock prices.

55:22.926 --> 55:26.476 align:middle
I can say that by some
valuation measures,

55:27.086 --> 55:30.566 align:middle
they're in the upper range of
their historical value ranges.

55:30.676 --> 55:36.186 align:middle
But, you know, I wouldn't want
to-I wouldn't want to speculate

55:36.186 --> 55:40.486 align:middle
about what the consequences of
a market correction should be.

55:40.486 --> 55:45.766 align:middle
You know, we would-we would look
very carefully at the nature

55:45.766 --> 55:50.236 align:middle
of it, and I mean,
it-really-really what hurts is

55:50.236 --> 55:54.446 align:middle
if consumers are borrowing
heavily and doing so against,

55:54.446 --> 55:56.806 align:middle
for example, an asset
that can fall in value.

55:56.806 --> 55:59.586 align:middle
So that's a really
serious matter

55:59.586 --> 56:02.926 align:middle
when you have a housing bubble
and highly levered consumers

56:03.156 --> 56:04.226 align:middle
and housing values fall.

56:04.226 --> 56:06.076 align:middle
And we know that that's
a really bad situation.

56:06.596 --> 56:11.396 align:middle
A simple drop in equity
prices is-all by itself,

56:11.566 --> 56:13.426 align:middle
doesn't really have
those features.

56:13.426 --> 56:16.456 align:middle
It could certainly feature-it
could certainly affect

56:16.986 --> 56:19.136 align:middle
consumption and have a negative
effect on the economy, though.

56:19.656 --> 56:21.176 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

56:21.596 --> 56:25.436 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

56:25.436 --> 56:27.516 align:middle
Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.com.

56:27.516 --> 56:30.886 align:middle
One thing that's changed since
the crisis is, the majority

56:30.886 --> 56:32.906 align:middle
of mortgages issued
last year came

56:32.906 --> 56:34.586 align:middle
from so-called nonbank lenders,

56:35.026 --> 56:36.946 align:middle
as traditional banks
scaled back.

56:36.946 --> 56:39.776 align:middle
What confidence do you
have about supervision and,

56:39.846 --> 56:42.976 align:middle
I suppose, also regulation
in this space given the role

56:42.976 --> 56:44.536 align:middle
of housing finance
in the crisis?

56:44.536 --> 56:44.866 align:middle
Thank you.

56:46.156 --> 56:46.506 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

56:46.506 --> 56:47.296 align:middle
It's a good question.

56:47.296 --> 56:48.576 align:middle
So you're right that a lot

56:48.576 --> 56:51.276 align:middle
of mortgages are now
originated outside the system.

56:51.506 --> 56:54.756 align:middle
Many of those, though, have to
meet certain basic standards

56:54.756 --> 56:56.196 align:middle
to be bought by the GSEs.

56:56.846 --> 57:00.536 align:middle
I think we-if we look at
mortgage credit more broadly,

57:00.536 --> 57:02.056 align:middle
though, what we see is

57:02.656 --> 57:05.966 align:middle
that credit is pretty
widely available to people

57:06.226 --> 57:10.636 align:middle
with high scores and with good
credit-good credit records,

57:11.136 --> 57:14.126 align:middle
and much, much less available
than it was before the crisis

57:14.476 --> 57:18.126 align:middle
to people with low scores and
perhaps troubled credit history.

57:19.016 --> 57:23.306 align:middle
You know, it's-have
we-have we set-have we got

57:23.306 --> 57:24.126 align:middle
that exactly right?

57:24.176 --> 57:24.936 align:middle
I don't know.

57:25.036 --> 57:27.146 align:middle
I mean, I-but that's
clearly-that's the reality.

57:27.216 --> 57:30.616 align:middle
In terms of those institutions,
they do have supervision

57:30.756 --> 57:34.896 align:middle
by the CFPB and other
state regulators.

57:35.586 --> 57:37.286 align:middle
But you're right.

57:37.326 --> 57:38.866 align:middle
They're much-much

57:38.866 --> 57:40.976 align:middle
of the origination process
has moved outside the banking

57:40.976 --> 57:42.236 align:middle
system, and it's
something we monitor.

57:43.286 --> 57:52.246 align:middle
September 26, 2018 Chairman
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

